window.GEAR_BOOK_CONTENTS["book-21"] = [{"id":1,"rawContent":"# 第 01 章　舊禮堂的鏡子\n\n九月的海風還帶著夏天的尾巴，吹過海聲國小的操場時，把升旗台上的國旗捲得啪啦啪啦響。\n\n放學鐘聲已經響過很久了。六年一班的教室裡，只剩一個男生還坐在位置上——他低著頭，把書包翻了一遍又一遍，臉色越來越白。\n\n「完了完了完了……我的功課呢？」\n\n他叫林澈，是六年一班最安靜的那種學生。成績中等，聲音不大，坐倒數第二排靠窗，平常不舉手，也很少被老師點名。要是你問班上同學「林澈是怎樣的人」，十個有八個會先愣一下，然後說：「呃……就，很安靜啊。」\n\n只有林澈自己知道，他其實不是在發呆。他只是在「看」。\n\n他喜歡看。看窗外榕樹上的鳥，看同學們下課時的笑鬧，看夕陽把整個教室染成橘色。他覺得這個世界有很多小地方，是別人都沒注意到的。\n\n比方說——他剛剛就注意到，教室後門那塊地磚，跟別的地磚顏色不太一樣。\n\n「找到了！」林澈從抽屜最裡面抽出被壓扁的聯絡簿，鬆了一口氣。\n\n他把聯絡簿塞進書包，正要走，忽然頓住。\n\n窗外那棵榕樹上，一隻鳥猛地飛起來，發出尖銳的叫聲。緊接著，林澈注意到——夕陽的光，好像「跳」了一下。\n\n怎麼說呢。就像有人拿著一支看不見的手電筒，在天空後面「唰」地掃過去。只是一瞬間，快得讓林澈以為是自己眼花。\n\n他搖搖頭，背起書包往外走。\n\n海聲國小放學後的走廊，安靜得連自己的腳步聲都特別清楚。林澈經過一樓盡頭時，腳步突然慢了下來。\n\n前面是舊禮堂。\n\n舊禮堂已經停用好幾年了。門口的木牌寫著「維修中，請勿進入」，鐵門也上了鎖。但此刻，那扇鐵門……開著一條縫。\n\n林澈本來想裝作沒看到。他可是個「安靜、不惹事」的孩子。\n\n可是，就在他經過鐵門的瞬間，從門縫裡透出一道「光」。\n\n那不是夕陽的光。夕陽是橘的、暖的。那道光是銀白色的，很淡很淡，像是從很遠很遠的地方滲出來的一樣，卻讓林澈的心臟「咚」地跳了一大下。\n\n他像被什麼東西牽著，鬼使神差地推開鐵門，走了進去。\n\n舊禮堂裡很暗，很悶，有一股木頭舊舊的味道。舞台上的布幕垂著，觀眾席的椅子蒙著厚厚的灰。林澈屏住呼吸，一步一步往舞台後方走。\n\n舞台後方，有一面鏡子。\n\n那是一面很大、很舊的鏡子，鑲在木框裡，鏡面蒙著灰，幾乎照不出人影。林澈記得，小時候曾經聽學長說，舊禮堂有一面「照不出人影的鏡子」，大家都當是鬼故事，沒人真的來看過。\n\n此刻，那面鏡子正發著銀白色的光。\n\n林澈往前走了兩步。\n\n鏡面上，灰塵像被看不見的手擦過一樣，一圈一圈地散開。然後，銀白色的光裡，浮現出一張臉。\n\n一張男孩的臉。\n\n那男孩看起來跟林澈差不多大。一樣的黑色短髮，一樣的眉毛，一樣的鼻子——簡直就像，鏡子裡的「林澈」。\n\n可是，又不一樣。\n\n鏡子裡的男孩在笑。不是那種淡淡的、禮貌的笑，而是眼睛亮亮的、好像隨時準備衝出去冒險的笑。鏡子裡的男孩，看起來……很快樂。\n\n林澈愣住了。\n\n就在這時，鏡子裡的男孩也發現了林澈。他睜大眼睛，露出「哇，我看到你了」的表情，然後抬起手，朝林澈揮了揮——\n\n「！」林澈往後退了一大步，腳跟撞到一張椅子，發出「匡噹」一聲巨響。\n\n等他回過神來再看，鏡面已經恢復原樣。只有灰，和一面照得出自己蒼白臉孔的老鏡子。\n\n銀白色的光消失了。就像從來沒有存在過。\n\n「呼……呼……」林澈大口喘著氣，心跳得像要從胸口跳出來。\n\n「你是誰？在裡面做什麼！」\n\n一個聲音突然從禮堂門口響起。林澈嚇得整個人跳起來。\n\n站在門口的，是六年一班的班長，周予晴。\n\n晴晴揹著書包，手裡抱著作業簿，皺著眉盯著他：「林澈？放學了你還待在舊禮堂？」她走進來，狐疑地左右看了看，「你一個人在這裡幹嘛？」\n\n「我、我……」林澈張了張嘴，不知道該怎麼說。說「我看到鏡子裡有個跟我長得一樣的男生在對我揮手」？這種話講出來，連他自己都覺得丟臉。\n\n「我只是……進來看一下。」他低下頭，小聲說。\n\n「看一下？」晴晴挑眉，「維修中的舊禮堂有什麼好看的？」\n\n林澈沒回答。\n\n晴晴盯著他看了好幾秒。她是那種「什麼事都要找出道理」的人，也是全班公認最難騙的人。她總覺得，世界上沒有莫名其妙的怪事，只有還沒被發現的原因。\n\n「算了。」晴晴最後說，「反正天快黑了，快點回家吧。這種地方，晚上一個人待著很危險。」\n\n她轉過身，走了兩步，又停下來。\n\n「咦？」\n\n「怎麼了？」林澈問。\n\n晴晴沒說話，只是盯著舞台後方那面鏡子看。\n\n「那面鏡子……有點奇怪。」她說，「你看，鏡子右下角，有一道細細的裂痕。可是剛才我在門口的時候，明明沒看到有裂痕。」\n\n林澈順著她的目光看過去。\n\n鏡子右下角，果然有一道極細的裂痕，細得像一根頭髮，幾乎看不見。\n\n可是——那道裂痕，是銀白色的。而且，正微微地、一閃一閃地發著光，像是……呼吸一樣。\n\n「哪有什麼裂痕。」林澈聽見自己說，「你是不是看錯了？」\n\n晴晴又看了好幾秒，最後「嗯」地一聲：「……可能吧。天黑了的關係。」\n\n她沒再看，揹著書包走出禮堂：「走吧，鎖門了。」\n\n林澈跟在她後面走出去。鐵門關上的那一刻，他回頭看了一眼。\n\n舊禮堂裡，那面鏡子的銀白色裂痕，還在他看不見的地方，一閃一閃地亮著。\n\n回家的路上，夕陽已經整個沉到海平面下去了。天邊還剩一點點橘紅色的光。\n\n林澈走著走著，忽然又想起一件事。\n\n開學第三天，好像是體育課。\n\n那天……那天發生過什麼事？\n\n他明明記得那天下午陽光很好，記得體育老師劉老師吹哨子的聲音，記得同學們在操場上跑來跑去。可是，再往後想，記憶就像被霧擋住了一樣，怎麼也想不起來。\n\n每次想到這裡，他的頭就隱隱地痛一下。\n\n「算了。」林澈低聲對自己說，「反正，一定不是什麼重要的事。」\n\n可是，那一夜，他躺在床上，閉上眼睛。\n\n黑暗裡，那張「跟他長得一樣、卻很快樂」的臉，又浮了上來。\n\n還有那句話，他總覺得自己好像聽過，卻怎麼也想不起是誰說的、在什麼時候說的——\n\n「你想試試看嗎？」\n\n窗外，海聲國小的方向，舊禮堂的屋頂上，一道細細的銀白色光芒，一閃，就消失了。\n","rawContentEn":"# Chapter 01 — The Mirror in the Old Auditorium\n\nSeptember sea breezes still carried the tail of summer. When they swept across Haisheng Elementary School's playground, they whipped the national flag on the flagpole until it snapped and flapped.\n\nThe school dismissal bell had rung a long time ago. In Classroom 6-1, only one boy was still sitting in his seat — head down, turning his backpack over and over, his face growing paler by the second.\n\n\"No, no, no... where's my homework?\"\n\nHis name was Lin Che, the quietest student in Class 6-1. Average grades, a soft voice, sitting by the window in the second-to-last row. He never raised his hand, and teachers rarely called on him. If you asked anyone in class, \"What kind of person is Lin Che?\", eight out of ten would pause, then say: \"Uh... he's, well, very quiet.\"\n\nOnly Lin Che himself knew the truth. He wasn't spacing out. He was \"watching.\"\n\nHe loved to watch. He watched the birds in the banyan tree outside the window, watched his classmates laugh and play at recess, watched the sunset paint the whole classroom orange. He felt the world was full of small details that other people never noticed.\n\nFor example — he'd just noticed that the floor tile behind the classroom's back door was a slightly different color from the others.\n\n\"Found it!\" Lin Che pulled his flattened home-communication notebook out from the very bottom of his desk drawer and let out a sigh of relief.\n\nHe stuffed the notebook into his backpack and was about to leave when he froze.\n\nOutside the window, a bird in the banyan tree suddenly burst into the air with a sharp cry. At the same moment, Lin Che noticed — the sunset light seemed to \"flicker.\"\n\nHow could he describe it? It was as if someone had swept an invisible flashlight across the sky behind everything. Just for a split second, so fast that Lin Che thought his eyes were playing tricks on him.\n\nHe shook his head, slung his backpack over his shoulder, and walked out.\n\nThe corridors of Haisheng Elementary after school were so quiet that his own footsteps echoed extra loudly. When he passed the end of the first-floor hallway, his steps slowed.\n\nIn front of him was the old auditorium.\n\nThe old auditorium had been out of use for years. A wooden sign on the door read, \"Under Renovation — Do Not Enter,\" and the iron gate was padlocked. But right now... that iron gate was open a crack.\n\nLin Che thought about pretending not to see it. After all, he was a \"quiet kid who never caused trouble.\"\n\nBut just as he passed the gate, a \"light\" seeped through the crack.\n\nIt wasn't sunset light. Sunset was orange and warm. This light was silvery-white, very faint, as if it were seeping in from somewhere very, very far away — and it made Lin Che's heart thump hard.\n\nAs if something were tugging at him, he pushed the iron gate open and stepped inside.\n\nThe old auditorium was dark and musty, smelling of old wood. The stage curtains hung still, and the audience seats were thick with dust. Holding his breath, Lin Che walked step by step toward the back of the stage.\n\nBehind the stage, there was a mirror.\n\nIt was a huge, ancient mirror set in a wooden frame, its surface so dusty it barely reflected a person. Lin Che remembered hearing from older students when he was little that the old auditorium had a \"mirror that couldn't show your reflection.\" Everyone treated it as a ghost story, and nobody had ever actually come to check.\n\nRight now, that mirror was glowing silvery-white.\n\nLin Che took two steps forward.\n\nOn the mirror's surface, the dust parted in rings, as if an invisible hand were wiping it away. Then, inside the silvery-white glow, a face appeared.\n\nA boy's face.\n\nThe boy looked about the same age as Lin Che. Same short black hair, same eyebrows, same nose — it was almost like the \"Lin Che\" in the mirror.\n\nBut it was also different.\n\nThe boy in the mirror was smiling. Not a faint, polite smile, but a smile with bright, sparkling eyes, like someone always ready to rush out on an adventure. The boy in the mirror looked... happy.\n\nLin Che froze.\n\nAt that very moment, the boy in the mirror spotted Lin Che too. He widened his eyes with a \"Oh wow, I can see you!\" expression, then raised his hand and waved at Lin Che—\n\n\"!\" Lin Che staggered back a full step, his heel banging into a chair with a loud CLANG.\n\nBy the time he came to his senses and looked again, the mirror had already returned to normal. Just dust, and an old mirror reflecting his own pale face.\n\nThe silvery-white light had vanished. As if it had never existed.\n\n\"Huff... huff...\" Lin Che panted, his heart pounding as if it would leap out of his chest.\n\n\"Who are you? What are you doing in here?!\"\n\nA voice suddenly rang out from the auditorium entrance. Lin Che jumped clean off the floor in fright.\n\nStanding at the door was Class 6-1's class monitor, Chou Yu-Ching.\n\nChing-Ching had her backpack on and homework notebooks in her arms. She frowned at him: \"Lin Che? School's over, why are you still in the old auditorium?\" She stepped inside and glanced around suspiciously. \"What are you doing in here all alone?\"\n\n\"I... I...\" Lin Che opened his mouth, not knowing what to say. Tell her \"I saw a boy who looks exactly like me waving at me in the mirror\"? Even he thought that sounded ridiculous out loud.\n\n\"I just... came in to take a look.\" He lowered his head and said quietly.\n\n\"Take a look?\" Ching-Ching raised an eyebrow. \"What's there to look at in an old auditorium under renovation?\"\n\nLin Che didn't answer.\n\nChing-Ching stared at him for a few long seconds. She was the kind of person who had to find a reason for everything — and the class's most universally acknowledged hardest person to fool. She always believed there were no mysterious strange things in the world, only causes that hadn't been discovered yet.\n\n\"Whatever.\" Ching-Ching finally said. \"It's getting dark anyway, hurry home. It's dangerous to be here alone at night.\"\n\nShe turned around, took two steps, then stopped.\n\n\"Huh?\"\n\n\"What's wrong?\" Lin Che asked.\n\nChing-Ching didn't answer. She just stared at the mirror behind the stage.\n\n\"That mirror... is a little strange,\" she said. \"Look, in the lower-right corner of the mirror, there's a thin crack. But when I was at the door just now, I definitely didn't see any crack.\"\n\nLin Che followed her gaze.\n\nIn the lower-right corner of the mirror, there was indeed an extremely thin crack — so fine it was like a single strand of hair, almost invisible.\n\nBut — that crack was silvery-white. And it was faintly pulsing with light, as if... it were breathing.\n\n\"There's no crack.\" Lin Che heard himself say. \"You must have seen wrong.\"\n\nChing-Ching stared a few more seconds, then let out an \"Mm...\": \"...Maybe. It's getting dark, that's all.\"\n\nShe didn't look again. Slinging her backpack on, she walked out of the auditorium: \"Come on, let's lock up.\"\n\nLin Che followed her out. The moment the iron gate closed, he glanced back.\n\nInside the old auditorium, in a place he couldn't see, the mirror's silvery-white crack was still faintly glowing, pulsing on and off.\n\nOn the way home, the sun had already sunk completely below the sea's horizon. A little orange-red glow still lingered at the edge of the sky.\n\nWalking along, Lin Che suddenly remembered something.\n\nThe third day of the new school year. It was PE class, he thought.\n\nThat day... what happened that day?\n\nHe clearly remembered the sunlight was wonderful that afternoon. He remembered the sound of Coach Liu blowing his whistle. He remembered his classmates running around the field. But when he tried to remember further, his memory was like it was blocked by fog — no matter how hard he tried, he couldn't recall.\n\nEvery time he got to this part, his head gave a faint, dull throb.\n\n\"Whatever.\" Lin Che muttered to himself. \"It probably wasn't anything important anyway.\"\n\nBut that night, lying in bed with his eyes closed —\n\nIn the darkness, that face — \"looking just like him, yet so happy\" — floated back up.\n\nAnd that sentence. He felt like he'd heard it somewhere before, but he couldn't remember who said it, or when —\n\n\"Don't you want to give it a try?\"\n\nOutside the window, in the direction of Haisheng Elementary School, on the roof of the old auditorium, a thin sliver of silvery-white light flickered once... and was gone.\n"},{"id":2,"rawContent":"# 第 02 章　海聲國小的怪事\n\n隔天早上，林澈頂著兩個黑眼圈走進校門。\n\n他整晚幾乎沒睡。只要一閉上眼睛，那面鏡子、那個笑容、那句「你想試試看嗎」，就會輪流在腦海裡轉。\n\n可是很奇怪，走進海聲國小的校門時，他發現自己不是唯一一個「怪怪的」人。\n\n校門口那家便利商店前面，圍著一群六年一班的同學，吵得不可開交。\n\n「我發誓！我發誓昨天放學的時候，這裡還是『海聲書店』！」阿寶揮著手，臉漲得通紅。\n\n「最好是啦！」小美反駁，「這裡一直都是小七啊，我每天都在這裡買養樂多！」\n\n「對啊對啊，阿寶你昨天是不是又熬夜打電動，打到頭殼壞去？」\n\n「我沒有！我真的記得！那個書店老闆娘還會送貼紙！」\n\n同學們笑成一團，拍拍阿寶的肩膀，一個一個走進校門。沒有人把他的話當真。\n\n只有林澈，站在原地，愣住了。\n\n因為他也記得。\n\n他記得開學那天，校門口那間店的招牌是藍色的，寫著「海聲書店」，玻璃門上貼著泛黃的書展海報。他記得自己還在那裡買過一支鉛筆。\n\n可是現在，站在他眼前的，是綠色的 7-ELEVEN 招牌，玻璃門上貼著咖啡買一送一的海報。\n\n好像，那間書店從來沒有存在過。\n\n「林澈，你在看什麼？」\n\n一個聲音從背後傳來。是班長晴晴。她抱著一疊作業簿，順著他的視線看過去。\n\n「沒有。」林澈搖搖頭。\n\n晴晴看了他一眼，沒說什麼，逕自走進校門。\n\n早自習的時候，林澈一直在想那件事。他偷偷往晴晴的座位看過去——\n\n晴晴低著頭，正在一本小筆記本上寫東西。她寫得很快，很專心，還不時停下來，用筆尖敲敲額頭。\n\n林澈悄悄挪動椅子，往她那邊靠了一點。\n\n筆記本上，工整的字跡寫著一行一行的紀錄：\n\n「9/17　舊禮堂鏡子出現裂痕，銀白色，會發光。」\n「9/17　林澈說他沒看到（說謊）。」\n「9/18　校門口商店：多數人記憶為 7-11，阿寶記憶為海聲書店。」\n\n林澈的心跳漏了一拍。\n\n晴晴忽然抬起頭，正好對上他的眼睛。\n\n「……你在偷看。」她說。\n\n「我、我沒有——」\n\n「有。」晴晴把筆記本蓋上，盯著他看，「而且我要更正昨天的紀錄。我不是『可能看錯』。我確實看到了那道裂痕，而且它在發光。」\n\n林澈愣住了：「那你昨天為什麼說……」\n\n「因為我不確定要不要相信自己的眼睛。」晴晴說得很坦然，「所以我回家查了資料。」\n\n她從抽屜裡拿出一張列印紙，壓低聲音：「海聲國小的網站上寫，舊禮堂在三十年前『因故停用』。可是，整份校史資料裡，都沒寫『故』是什麼。」\n\n「三十年？」林澈瞪大眼睛。\n\n「嗯。」晴晴點頭，眼睛裡有一種林澈很熟悉的光——那是她每次要解數學難題時的眼神，「所以，有兩件事很可疑。第一，那面鏡子。第二，大家的記憶。」\n\n她頓了一下。\n\n「林澈，你也記得那間書店，對不對？」\n\n林澈張了張嘴，最後點了點頭。\n\n晴晴看著他，沒有笑他，也沒有說「你怎麼可能記得」。她只是翻開筆記本，在最後一行寫下：\n\n「林澈：記得海聲書店。」\n\n然後，她把筆記本收進書包，小聲說：「放學後，我們再去一次舊禮堂。」\n\n「我們？」\n\n「對。」晴晴看了他一眼，「兩個人，比較不會怕。」\n\n話才剛說完，一顆頭突然從他們兩個中間冒出來。\n\n「你們在講什麼悄悄話？我也要聽！」\n\n是阿達。\n\n吳達，六年一班的點子王。他頭髮永遠有一撮翹起來，制服永遠紮一半，抽屜裡永遠藏著各種奇怪的東西——橡皮筋、彈珠、不知道從哪撿來的彈簧。他鬼點子最多，膽子最大，也最吵。\n\n「沒有啦。」晴晴皺眉。\n\n「有！我聽到了！舊禮堂對不對？」阿達眼睛一亮，「跟你們講一個更猛的——我昨天夢到林澈耶！」\n\n「你夢到我？」林澈心裡一緊。\n\n「對啊！我夢到你在舊禮堂，跟一個……跟你長得一樣的人講話。」阿達比手畫腳，「我本來要叫你，結果你們兩個一起轉過來看我，我就嚇醒了。」\n\n班上幾個同學聽到，紛紛湊過來笑：「阿達又開始了。」「你昨天是不是看鬼片？」「阿達的夢可以拍電影了啦。」\n\n「吼，你們很煩耶，我是說真的！」阿達抗議。\n\n笑鬧聲中，阿達忽然盯著林澈，歪著頭，像在想什麼。\n\n「而且啊，」他說，「我好像在鏡子裡看過你。」\n\n空氣安靜了一秒。\n\n阿達隨即自己笑了出來：「哈哈，開玩笑的啦，不然你是鏡子妖怪喔？」\n\n大家又笑成一團，上課鐘聲正好響起。同學們一哄而散。\n\n只有林澈，站在原地，手心全是冷汗。\n\n放學後，四點整。\n\n太陽斜斜地掛在操場邊，把三個人的影子拉得好長。林澈、晴晴，還有硬要跟來的阿達，站在舊禮堂的鐵門前。\n\n「門鎖著耶。」阿達伸手推了推。\n\n「昨天是開的。」林澈說。\n\n晴晴走上前，蹲下來，仔細看那道鐵門。\n\n「門鎖沒有被破壞。」她說，「可是——門縫裡有光。」\n\n三個人同時屏住呼吸。\n\n從鐵門的縫隙裡，滲出一線淡淡的銀白色光芒。跟昨天一模一樣。\n\n「推開。」晴晴說。\n\n三個人一起用力，「嘎——」的一聲，鐵門緩緩推開。\n\n禮堂裡，一切如常。灰塵、布幕、蒙著布的椅子。\n\n然後，他們看到了。\n\n舞台後方那面老鏡子，正在發光。\n\n不是昨天那種微微的、若有似無的光。今天，鏡面像被丟進一顆石子的水面，一圈一圈地泛起漣漪。銀白色的波紋從鏡子中央擴散開來，越擴越大，越擴越快。\n\n「哇……」阿達張大嘴巴，「這是什麼酷東西？」\n\n林澈卻往前走了兩步。\n\n在盪漾的波紋裡，他隱約看見——不是自己的倒影。\n\n是「另一個地方」。\n\n那個地方，看起來很像一間教室。有黑板，有桌椅，有窗戶。黑板上有粉筆字，窗台上擺著盆栽。\n\n可是，又不太一樣。\n\n教室後面的佈告欄，貼滿了畫，滿到快要掉下來。窗邊掛著一串貝殼風鈴，正在輕輕地轉。\n\n「那是……我們的教室嗎？」阿達小聲問。\n\n「不是。」晴晴搖頭，「我們的佈告欄是公布欄，沒有畫。」\n\n就在這時候，鏡子裡的畫面，動了。\n\n一個女生的背影，從教室後面走過去。她穿著海聲國小的制服，馬尾晃啊晃的，走到佈告欄前面停了下來。\n\n然後，她好像聽到了什麼，回過頭——\n\n林澈他們三個，同時倒抽了一口氣。\n\n那個女生，是晴晴。\n\n又不是晴晴。\n\n她跟晴晴長得一模一樣，可是，她制服上的別針，別在右邊胸口。晴晴的別針，別在左邊。\n\n而且，她在笑。是一種很輕鬆、很開心的笑——那是一種，六年一班的同學從來沒有在晴晴臉上見過的笑。\n\n鏡子裡的「晴晴」，看著他們三個。\n\n她的嘴巴慢慢張開，像是想說什麼。\n\n波紋忽然變強，銀白色的光整個亮了起來，刺得三個人睜不開眼睛。\n\n然後，在光芒的最深處，他們聽見了一個聲音。\n\n一個女生的聲音，隔著很遠很遠的距離，帶著驚訝，從鏡子的另一邊傳來——\n\n「你們……看得到我們嗎？」\n","rawContentEn":"# Chapter 02 — The Strange Happenings at Haisheng Elementary\n\nThe next morning, Lin Che walked through the school gate with two dark circles under his eyes.\n\nHe'd barely slept all night. Every time he closed his eyes, the mirror, that smile, and the words \"Don't you want to give it a try?\" took turns spinning through his head.\n\nBut strangely enough, when he walked through Haisheng Elementary's gate, he realized he wasn't the only one acting \"off.\"\n\nIn front of the convenience store by the school gate, a crowd of Class 6-1 students had gathered, arguing fiercely.\n\n\"I swear! I swear, when school let out yesterday, this place was still 'Haisheng Bookstore'!\" A-Bao waved his arms, his face flushed red.\n\n\"As if!\" Xiao-Mei shot back. \"This has always been a 7-Eleven! I buy my Yakult here every single day!\"\n\n\"Yeah, yeah, A-Bao, did you stay up all night gaming again and fry your brain?\"\n\n\"I didn't! I really remember! The bookstore lady even gave out stickers!\"\n\nThe classmates burst out laughing, patted A-Bao on the shoulder, and filed through the gate one by one. Nobody took him seriously.\n\nOnly Lin Che stood frozen in place.\n\nBecause he remembered too.\n\nHe remembered that on the first day of school, the sign on that store by the gate was blue and read \"Haisheng Bookstore,\" with a yellowed book-fair poster taped to the glass door. He remembered buying a pencil there.\n\nBut now, what stood before his eyes was a green 7-Eleven sign, with a buy-one-get-one coffee poster on the glass door.\n\nIt was as if that bookstore had never existed.\n\n\"Lin Che, what are you looking at?\"\n\nA voice came from behind him. It was the class monitor, Ching-Ching. She was carrying a stack of homework notebooks and followed his gaze.\n\n\"Nothing.\" Lin Che shook his head.\n\nChing-Ching glanced at him, said nothing, and walked through the gate on her own.\n\nDuring morning self-study, Lin Che kept thinking about it. He secretly glanced toward Ching-Ching's seat—\n\nChing-Ching had her head down, writing in a small notebook. She wrote quickly and intently, occasionally pausing to tap her forehead with the tip of her pen.\n\nLin Che quietly shifted his chair a little closer to her.\n\nIn the notebook, neat handwriting filled line after line:\n\n\"9/17 — Crack appears in the old auditorium mirror. Silvery-white. Glows.\"\n\"9/17 — Lin Che says he didn't see it (lying).\"\n\"9/18 — Store by the school gate: most people remember 7-Eleven, A-Bao remembers Haisheng Bookstore.\"\n\nLin Che's heart skipped a beat.\n\nChing-Ching suddenly looked up, meeting his eyes exactly.\n\n\"...You're peeking,\" she said.\n\n\"I— I wasn't—\"\n\n\"You were.\" Ching-Ching closed the notebook and stared at him. \"And I need to correct yesterday's record. I didn't 'maybe see wrong.' I definitely saw that crack, and it was glowing.\"\n\nLin Che was stunned: \"Then why did you say yesterday...\"\n\n\"Because I wasn't sure if I should trust my own eyes.\" Ching-Ching said it plainly. \"So I went home and looked things up.\"\n\nShe pulled a printout from her desk and lowered her voice: \"Haisheng Elementary's website says the old auditorium was 'closed for certain reasons' thirty years ago. But nowhere in the entire school history does it say what those 'certain reasons' were.\"\n\n\"Thirty years?\" Lin Che's eyes went wide.\n\n\"Mm.\" Ching-Ching nodded, a certain light in her eyes — one Lin Che knew well. It was the look she got whenever she was about to crack a tough math problem. \"So there are two suspicious things. One, that mirror. Two, everyone's memories.\"\n\nShe paused.\n\n\"Lin Che, you remember that bookstore too, don't you?\"\n\nLin Che opened his mouth, then finally nodded.\n\nChing-Ching looked at him — didn't laugh at him, didn't say \"how could you possibly remember that.\" She just opened her notebook and wrote on the last line:\n\n\"Lin Che: remembers Haisheng Bookstore.\"\n\nThen she tucked the notebook into her backpack and said quietly, \"After school, let's go to the old auditorium again.\"\n\n\"We?\"\n\n\"Yes.\" Ching-Ching glanced at him. \"Two people. Less scary.\"\n\nThe words had barely left her mouth when a head suddenly popped up between the two of them.\n\n\"What are you two whispering about? I want in!\"\n\nIt was A-Da.\n\nWu Da, Class 6-1's idea machine. He always had a tuft of hair sticking up, his uniform always half-tucked, and his desk drawer always stuffed with strange things — rubber bands, marbles, springs picked up from who-knows-where. He had the wildest ideas, the biggest guts, and the loudest voice.\n\n\"Nothing.\" Ching-Ching frowned.\n\n\"There was! I heard you! The old auditorium, right?\" A-Da's eyes lit up. \"Then let me tell you something even crazier — I dreamed about Lin Che last night!\"\n\n\"You dreamed about me?\" Lin Che tensed.\n\n\"Yeah! I dreamed you were in the old auditorium, talking to a... a guy who looked just like you.\" A-Da gestured wildly. \"I was about to call out to you, but then you both turned around and looked at me at the same time, and I woke up scared.\"\n\nA few classmates overheard and crowded over, laughing: \"There goes A-Da again.\" \"Did you watch a horror movie last night?\" \"A-Da's dreams could be a movie, seriously.\"\n\n\"Ugh, you guys are the worst, I'm telling the truth!\" A-Da protested.\n\nAmid the laughter, A-Da suddenly stared at Lin Che, tilting his head as if thinking about something.\n\n\"Also,\" he said, \"I think I've seen you in a mirror before.\"\n\nThe air went quiet for a second.\n\nThen A-Da burst out laughing at his own joke: \"Haha, just kidding! What are you, a mirror monster?\"\n\nEveryone laughed again as the class bell rang. The students scattered.\n\nOnly Lin Che stood where he was, his palms slick with cold sweat.\n\nAfter school, four o'clock sharp.\n\nThe sun hung low at the edge of the playground, stretching the three of them into long shadows. Lin Che, Ching-Ching, and A-Da — who had insisted on tagging along — stood before the old auditorium's iron gate.\n\n\"It's locked.\" A-Da reached out and pushed.\n\n\"It was open yesterday,\" Lin Che said.\n\nChing-Ching stepped forward, crouched down, and examined the gate carefully.\n\n\"The lock hasn't been tampered with,\" she said. \"But — there's light in the gap.\"\n\nAll three of them held their breath at the same time.\n\nFrom the gap in the iron gate seeped a faint thread of silvery-white light. Exactly like yesterday.\n\n\"Push it open,\" Ching-Ching said.\n\nThe three of them pushed together. With a long creak, the iron gate slowly swung open.\n\nInside the auditorium, everything was as usual. Dust, curtains, sheet-covered chairs.\n\nThen they saw it.\n\nBehind the stage, that old mirror was glowing.\n\nNot the faint, barely-there glow from yesterday. Today, the mirror's surface rippled in rings, like water after a stone had been dropped in. Silvery-white waves spread out from the center of the mirror, growing wider, faster and faster.\n\n\"Whoa...\" A-Da's mouth fell open. \"What is this cool thing?\"\n\nBut Lin Che had already taken two steps forward.\n\nIn the rippling waves, he vaguely saw — not his own reflection.\n\nIt was \"somewhere else.\"\n\nThat place looked a lot like a classroom. A blackboard, desks, chairs, windows. Chalk writing on the board, potted plants on the windowsill.\n\nBut it was also different.\n\nThe bulletin board at the back of that classroom was covered in drawings, so many they were about to fall off. A string of shell wind chimes hung by the window, turning gently.\n\n\"Is that... our classroom?\" A-Da whispered.\n\n\"No.\" Ching-Ching shook her head. \"Our board is a notice board. There are no drawings.\"\n\nJust then, the image in the mirror moved.\n\nThe back of a girl walked past the back of the classroom. She wore a Haisheng Elementary uniform, her ponytail swinging, and stopped in front of the bulletin board.\n\nThen, as if she'd heard something, she turned around—\n\nLin Che and the others gasped at the same time.\n\nThat girl was Ching-Ching.\n\nAnd she wasn't Ching-Ching.\n\nShe looked exactly like Ching-Ching, but the pin on her uniform was on the right side of her chest. Ching-Ching's pin was on the left.\n\nAnd she was smiling. A relaxed, happy smile — the kind of smile that no one in Class 6-1 had ever seen on Ching-Ching's face.\n\nThe \"Ching-Ching\" in the mirror looked at the three of them.\n\nHer mouth slowly opened, as if she wanted to say something.\n\nThe ripples suddenly intensified. The silvery-white light flared up, so bright the three of them had to squeeze their eyes shut.\n\nThen, from the deepest part of the light, they heard a voice.\n\nA girl's voice, from very, very far away, on the other side of the mirror, carrying surprise—\n\n\"Can you... can you see us?\"\n"},{"id":3,"rawContent":"# 第 03 章　鏡子裡的六年一班\n\n那句聲音像一顆小石子，丟進三個人的心裡，咚。\n\n禮堂裡安靜得只剩下心跳聲。銀白色的光，在鏡面上緩緩流動。\n\n「她、她是在跟我們說話嗎？」阿達的聲音有點抖。\n\n晴晴深吸一口氣，往前跨了一步。她是班長，遇到事情，永遠第一個站出來。\n\n她朝著鏡子，大聲說：「看得到！我們看得到你們！」\n\n鏡子裡的畫面震了一下。\n\n那個「另一個晴晴」睜大眼睛，轉頭朝教室裡大喊：「快過來！他們聽得到！」\n\n教室裡，好幾個同學立刻跑了過來。\n\n畫面越來越清楚——那不是一面鏡子裡的倒影，而像是一扇「窗戶」。\n\n窗戶的另一邊，是一間教室。跟他們的海聲國小六年一班很像：一樣的黑板、一樣的課桌椅、一樣的窗戶。\n\n可是，到處都不一樣。\n\n教室後面的佈告欄貼滿了畫，五顏六色，熱鬧得像一座小花園。窗邊掛著一串貝殼風鈴，正在輕輕地轉。講台上，放著一盆長得很好的黃金葛。\n\n而且，他們的制服——\n\n晴晴的視線銳利地掃過去。\n\n「他們的別針，別在右邊。」她低聲說。\n\n林澈這才注意到：窗戶另一邊的每一個人，制服上的別針都別在右胸。而他們自己的，別在左胸。\n\n鏡子另一邊的孩子們，也隔著「窗戶」看著他們。兩邊的人，你看我、我看你，誰都沒有說話。\n\n那種感覺很奇怪。就像走進一間四面都是鏡子的房間，鏡子裡的每一個人，都似曾相識。\n\n阿達忽然指著對面：「等一下！那個……那個是我嗎？」\n\n窗戶另一邊，有個男生正站在人群中間。他的頭髮也有一撮翹起來，制服也紮一半，可是——\n\n他的姿態很挺，身邊圍著三四個同學，大家好像在聽他說話。他一邊講，一邊比手畫腳，講到一半還自己先笑出來。\n\n旁邊的同學拍拍他的肩膀，喊了一聲：「達哥！」\n\n「達哥？」阿達瞪大眼睛，「他、他怎麼有綽號？」\n\n晴晴沒理他，目光在對面的教室裡搜尋，最後停在教室正中央。\n\n那裡坐著一個男生。他坐得很直，下巴微微抬著，正在跟旁邊的同學說話。旁邊的同學一邊聽一邊笑，還有人特地轉過頭來看他。\n\n那個男生，是林澈。\n\n又不是林澈。\n\n他留著一樣的短髮，有著一樣的眉眼。可是，他臉上的表情，是林澈從來沒有在自己臉上見過的——\n\n自信，開朗，像太陽一樣，會發光。\n\n鏡子裡的男孩，好像感應到什麼，抬起頭，往「窗戶」這邊看了過來。\n\n四目相接的那一刻，林澈的心臟猛地一縮。\n\n那張臉，他見過。就在昨天晚上，在鏡子的波紋裡，朝他揮手的那張臉。\n\n鏡子裡的男孩愣了一下，然後——笑了。\n\n他抬起手，朝著林澈，揮了揮。\n\n一模一樣的動作。\n\n「你認識他喔？」阿達湊過來，小聲問。\n\n林澈沒有回答。他覺得自己的喉嚨，好像被什麼東西卡住了。\n\n晴晴往前走了一步，隔著「窗戶」，對著對面的晴晴喊：「你們……也是海聲國小六年一班？」\n\n對面的晴晴點頭。她的聲音穿過鏡面，聽起來有點像隔著一層水：「對。你們也是？」\n\n「對。」\n\n兩個長得一模一樣的女孩，隔著一面鏡子，互相看著對方。\n\n「今天幾月幾號？」晴晴問。\n\n「九月十八號。」對面的晴晴回答，「你們呢？」\n\n「……九月十八號。」\n\n兩邊同時安靜了下來。\n\n同一天。同一所學校。同一個班級。\n\n晴晴皺起眉頭。她的大腦正在飛快地運轉——如果兩邊是同一天、同一所學校、同一個班級，那為什麼，兩邊的班級會長得這麼不一樣？\n\n「你們那邊……」對面的晴晴忽然開口，語氣有點遲疑，「最近有沒有發生什麼奇怪的事？」\n\n「有。」晴晴毫不猶豫地回答，「校門口的店，有人記得是書店，有人記得是便利商店。」\n\n對面的晴晴瞪大眼睛：「我們也是！而且——在我們這裡，那家店一直是海聲書店，可是我們班有人記得，它本來是 7-11。」\n\n「7-11？」晴晴立刻追問，「誰記得？」\n\n「阿寶。」對面的晴晴說，「他信誓旦旦說，他每天都在那家 7-11 買養樂多。可是我們這裡，明明一直都是書店。」\n\n晴晴低下頭，從口袋裡拿出小筆記本，飛快地寫下幾行字。她的手在發抖，可是字還是寫得整整齊齊。\n\n「所以，兩邊世界的記憶，都開始『對不上』了。」她喃喃地說。\n\n就在這時候，禮堂外面傳來腳步聲。\n\n三個人同時僵住。\n\n是學校的工友伯伯。他推著清潔車，從禮堂門口經過。\n\n他往禮堂裡看了一眼。\n\n三個人屏住呼吸——他們就站在那裡，站在發光的鏡子前面。這麼大的動靜，不可能看不見。\n\n可是工友伯伯只是打了個哈欠，自言自語：「這間禮堂喔，什麼時候要拆一拆……」\n\n然後，他就推著車走了。\n\n從頭到尾，他都沒有看見他們。也沒有看見那道銀白色的光。\n\n「他……沒看到我們？」阿達的聲音發抖。\n\n晴晴站在原地，臉色發白，卻很鎮定：「不是沒看到我們。是沒看到『這裡發生的事』。」\n\n林澈忽然想起昨天的自己——如果昨天晴晴沒有走進來，他是不是也會像工友伯伯一樣，什麼都看不見？\n\n鏡子另一邊，對面的晴晴著急地喊：「喂！你們還在嗎？」\n\n「在！」晴晴回過神，大聲回答。\n\n可是，鏡面上的波紋，開始變快了。\n\n銀白色的光一閃一閃，畫面像水中的倒影一樣晃動起來。對面教室的聲音，也變得忽遠忽近。\n\n「好像……要關起來了！」林澈說。\n\n「明天！」對面的晴晴把雙手貼在鏡面上，大聲喊，「明天同一時間，你們再來！」\n\n「好！」晴晴用力點頭。\n\n鏡子裡，那個「另一個林澈」也往前走了一步。\n\n他隔著越來越模糊的畫面，對著林澈，做了一個嘴型。\n\n林澈讀出來了。\n\n那個嘴型是：「明天見。」\n\n下一秒，銀白色的光整個收縮、消失。\n\n鏡面恢復成一面蒙著灰的老鏡子。\n\n禮堂裡，只剩下三個孩子的呼吸聲。\n\n「……我們剛才，是不是在跟『另一個自己』講話？」阿達終於開口，聲音還有點抖。\n\n「嗯。」晴晴把筆記本收好，站起來，「而且，我大概知道明天要做什麼了。」\n\n「做什麼？」林澈問。\n\n「過去。」晴晴看著鏡子，眼神很亮，「如果要弄清楚這一切，我們就得過去看看。」\n\n阿達立刻跳起來：「我要去！我一定要去！」\n\n林澈看著那面鏡子。鏡子裡的自己，蒼白、安靜、縮著肩膀。\n\n他想起那個「另一個自己」的笑容。\n\n他想起那句，他到現在還想不起來的「你想試試看嗎」。\n\n「我也去。」他聽見自己說。\n\n三個人走出舊禮堂的時候，夕陽已經快要沉進海裡了。\n\n沒有人注意到，鏡子右下角那道銀白色的裂痕，比昨天——\n\n又長了一點。\n","rawContentEn":"# Chapter 03 — Class 6-1 in the Mirror\n\nThat voice landed like a small pebble dropped into the hearts of all three of them. Plop.\n\nInside the auditorium, it was so quiet that only their heartbeats could be heard. The silvery-white light flowed slowly across the mirror's surface.\n\n\"Is... is she talking to us?\" A-Da's voice trembled a little.\n\nChing-Ching took a deep breath and stepped forward. She was the class monitor — when something happened, she was always the first to step up.\n\nShe raised her voice toward the mirror: \"We can see you! We can see you guys!\"\n\nThe image in the mirror shuddered.\n\nThe \"other Ching-Ching\" widened her eyes and turned to shout into the classroom: \"Come quick! They can hear us!\"\n\nInside the classroom, several students immediately came running.\n\nThe image grew clearer and clearer — it wasn't a reflection in a mirror, but rather like a \"window.\"\n\nOn the other side of the window was a classroom. It looked a lot like their Class 6-1 at Haisheng Elementary: same blackboard, same desks and chairs, same windows.\n\nBut everywhere, things were different.\n\nThe bulletin board at the back of the classroom was covered in drawings, colorful and lively, like a little garden. A string of shell wind chimes hung by the window, turning gently. On the teacher's desk sat a thriving pothos plant.\n\nAnd their uniforms—\n\nChing-Ching's gaze swept over sharply.\n\n\"Their pins are on the right,\" she said quietly.\n\nOnly then did Lin Che notice: everyone on the other side of the window wore their uniform pin on the right side of their chest. Their own were on the left.\n\nThe children on the other side of the mirror were also looking at them through the \"window.\" Both sides stared at each other, and nobody said a word.\n\nIt was a strange feeling. Like walking into a room with mirrors on all four walls — everyone in the mirror looked vaguely familiar.\n\nA-Da suddenly pointed across: \"Wait! That... is that me?\"\n\nOn the other side of the window, a boy stood in the middle of a crowd. His hair also had a tuft sticking up, his uniform was also half-tucked, but—\n\nHe stood tall, with three or four classmates gathered around him, as if listening to him talk. He gestured as he spoke, and halfway through, he laughed at his own joke first.\n\nA classmate beside him patted his shoulder and called out: \"Da-ge!\"\n\n\"Da-ge?\" A-Da's eyes went wide. \"He— he has a nickname?\"\n\nChing-Ching ignored him. Her gaze swept the classroom across the way and finally stopped at the very center.\n\nA boy sat there. He sat up straight, chin slightly raised, talking with the classmate next to him. The classmate laughed as he listened, and someone even turned around specifically to look at him.\n\nThat boy was Lin Che.\n\nAnd he wasn't Lin Che.\n\nHe had the same short hair, the same brows and eyes. But the expression on his face was one that Lin Che had never seen on his own face—\n\nConfident, bright, like the sun. Glowing.\n\nThe boy in the mirror seemed to sense something. He looked up, straight toward the \"window.\"\n\nThe moment their eyes met, Lin Che's heart clenched tight.\n\nThat face — he'd seen it before. Just last night, in the ripples of the mirror. The face that had waved at him.\n\nThe boy in the mirror paused for a moment, then— smiled.\n\nHe raised his hand toward Lin Che and waved.\n\nExactly the same gesture.\n\n\"You know him?\" A-Da leaned in and whispered.\n\nLin Che didn't answer. He felt like something was stuck in his throat.\n\nChing-Ching stepped forward and shouted through the \"window\" at the other Ching-Ching: \"Are you... also Class 6-1 of Haisheng Elementary?\"\n\nThe other Ching-Ching nodded. Her voice came through the mirror's surface sounding a bit like it was passing through water: \"Yes. You too?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nThe two identical-looking girls looked at each other across the mirror.\n\n\"What's today's date?\" Ching-Ching asked.\n\n\"September 18th,\" the other Ching-Ching answered. \"You?\"\n\n\"...September 18th.\"\n\nBoth sides fell silent at the same time.\n\nThe same day. The same school. The same class.\n\nChing-Ching frowned. Her brain was spinning fast — if both sides were the same day, the same school, the same class, then why did the two classes look so completely different?\n\n\"On your side...\" the other Ching-Ching suddenly spoke, her tone a little hesitant, \"has anything strange happened recently?\"\n\n\"Yes.\" Ching-Ching answered without hesitation. \"The store by the school gate — some people remember it as a bookstore, some remember it as a convenience store.\"\n\nThe other Ching-Ching's eyes went wide: \"Us too! And — here, that store has always been Haisheng Bookstore, but someone in our class remembers it being a 7-Eleven.\"\n\n\"A 7-Eleven?\" Ching-Ching immediately pressed. \"Who remembers that?\"\n\n\"A-Bao.\" The other Ching-Ching said. \"He swears he buys Yakult at that 7-Eleven every day. But here, it's always been a bookstore.\"\n\nChing-Ching lowered her head, pulled the small notebook from her pocket, and wrote several lines quickly. Her hand was shaking, but her writing stayed neat.\n\n\"So the memories of both worlds are starting to 'not match up,'\" she murmured.\n\nJust then, footsteps came from outside the auditorium.\n\nAll three of them froze.\n\nIt was the school's janitor. He pushed his cleaning cart past the auditorium entrance.\n\nHe glanced inside.\n\nThe three of them held their breath — they were standing right there, right in front of the glowing mirror. There was no way he could miss them.\n\nBut the janitor only yawned and muttered to himself: \"This old auditorium... when are they ever going to tear it down...\"\n\nThen he pushed his cart and walked away.\n\nFrom beginning to end, he never saw them. And he never saw the silvery-white light.\n\n\"He... didn't see us?\" A-Da's voice trembled.\n\nChing-Ching stood where she was, face pale but calm: \"It's not that he didn't see us. It's that he didn't see what's happening here.\"\n\nLin Che suddenly thought of himself yesterday — if Ching-Ching hadn't walked in yesterday, would he have been just like the janitor, seeing nothing at all?\n\nOn the other side of the mirror, the other Ching-Ching called out anxiously: \"Hey! Are you still there?\"\n\n\"Yes!\" Ching-Ching snapped back to attention and answered loudly.\n\nBut the ripples on the mirror's surface were speeding up.\n\nThe silvery-white light flickered, and the image swayed like a reflection in water. The sounds from the classroom across the way grew distant, then near, then distant again.\n\n\"It's... it's closing!\" Lin Che said.\n\n\"Tomorrow!\" The other Ching-Ching pressed both hands against the mirror and shouted, \"Come back tomorrow, same time!\"\n\n\"Okay!\" Ching-Ching nodded hard.\n\nIn the mirror, the \"other Lin Che\" also stepped forward.\n\nThrough the blurring image, he mouthed something at Lin Che.\n\nLin Che read it.\n\nIt was: \"See you tomorrow.\"\n\nThe next second, the silvery-white light contracted and vanished.\n\nThe mirror returned to being an old, dust-covered mirror.\n\nIn the auditorium, only the breathing of three children remained.\n\n\"...Did we just talk to 'another us'?\" A-Da finally spoke, his voice still shaking.\n\n\"Mm.\" Ching-Ching put her notebook away and stood up. \"And I think I know what we're doing tomorrow.\"\n\n\"What?\" Lin Che asked.\n\n\"Going over.\" Ching-Ching looked at the mirror, her eyes bright. \"If we want to figure all this out, we have to go over and take a look.\"\n\nA-Da jumped to his feet at once: \"I'm going! I'm definitely going!\"\n\nLin Che looked at the mirror. His reflection there was pale, quiet, shoulders hunched.\n\nHe thought of the smile on that \"other him.\"\n\nHe thought of that sentence he still couldn't remember: \"Don't you want to give it a try?\"\n\n\"I'm going too,\" he heard himself say.\n\nBy the time the three of them walked out of the old auditorium, the sun was about to sink into the sea.\n\nNo one noticed that the silvery-white crack in the lower-right corner of the mirror had grown—\n\na little longer than yesterday.\n"},{"id":4,"rawContent":"# 第 04 章　平行世界的我們\n\n九月十九號，星期三。\n\n這天早上，六年一班有三個人特別不對勁。\n\n晴晴的聯絡簿忘了給媽媽簽。阿達在早自習偷笑了三次，被老師罰站。林澈則是一直盯著窗外，連他最喜歡的那隻停在榕樹上的白頭翁，都沒能讓他回神。\n\n因為他們的心，全都留在舊禮堂那面鏡子裡。\n\n早自習的時候，教室裡很吵。予晴站起來，拍了一下桌子：「安靜！早自習要安靜！」\n\n全班立刻安靜了三秒，然後又嗡嗡嗡地吵起來。\n\n阿達講了一個笑話，幾個同學笑了兩聲，馬上又被予晴瞪回去：「吳達，安靜。」\n\n阿達摸摸鼻子，坐了下來。\n\n林澈看著這一切，忽然想起昨天鏡子裡的那個「另一個阿達」——被大家圍著、被叫「達哥」的那個阿達。\n\n「如果……」\n\n他趕緊搖搖頭，把這個念頭甩掉。\n\n放學後，三點五十分。三個人先在教室後面集合。\n\n晴晴攤開筆記本，像在念作戰計畫：「先確認規則。第一，鏡子四點五分開，四點二十分關。第二，我們一定要在關之前回來。第三——」\n\n「第三，不能亂碰『另一個自己』。」阿達搶著說。\n\n「你怎麼知道？」晴晴瞪他。\n\n「我猜的。」阿達理直氣壯，「電影都這樣演。」\n\n林澈從口袋裡拿出一支鉛筆。藍色的筆身，上面印著「海聲書店」四個字。\n\n「你帶鉛筆幹嘛？」阿達問。\n\n「……沒什麼。」林澈把鉛筆握緊了一點，「只是覺得，應該帶著。」\n\n四點整，三個人站在舊禮堂的鐵門前。這次，鐵門是開的。\n\n禮堂裡，那面鏡子已經開始發光。銀白色的波紋一圈一圈盪開，像有人在水面下點了一盞燈。\n\n而鏡子的另一邊，已經站著一群孩子。\n\nB 世界的人，早就等在那裡了。\n\n「他們來了！」對面的晴晴大喊。她今天綁著馬尾，笑容亮亮的，制服別針別在右胸。\n\n「走吧。」晴晴深吸一口氣，率先走向鏡子。\n\n「等等等等——」阿達突然拉住她，「要先進去的話，應該是我吧？我比較勇敢。」\n\n「你只是比較吵。」晴晴說。\n\n「那也是一種勇敢！」\n\n最後，阿達第一個走。他站在鏡子前面，閉上眼睛，大喊：「衝啊——」\n\n然後，他整個人「陷」進鏡面裡，像走進一扇沒有門的門。\n\n林澈親眼看見阿達的背影消失在銀白色的光裡。他愣了一秒，趕緊跟上。\n\n穿越的感覺，很難形容。\n\n像是走進一池涼涼的水裡。耳朵先是一陣嗡嗡的耳鳴，接著，所有聲音都消失了——聽不到風聲、聽不到蟬聲、聽不到自己的心跳。\n\n然後，啪。\n\n他睜開眼睛。\n\n他站在舊禮堂裡。\n\n一樣的灰塵，一樣的布幕，一樣的老鏡子。可是——空氣的味道不一樣了。原本的舊禮堂有一股悶悶的霉味，這裡卻有一股淡淡的、曬過太陽的味道。\n\n「哇——」阿達的聲音從前面傳來，「你們快出來看！」\n\n三個人走出禮堂。\n\n然後，他們全部愣住了。\n\n這裡是海聲國小。\n\n一樣的紅磚圍牆，一樣的穿堂，一樣的操場。可是——\n\n「溜滑梯！」阿達指著操場後面，大叫。\n\n操場後面，有一座溜滑梯。藍色的溜滑梯，看起來有點舊，卻閃閃發亮，旁邊還圍著幾個低年級的小朋友，正在排隊。\n\n林澈愣住了。他們學校操場後面，明明只有一片空地。\n\n可是在這裡，溜滑梯就站在那裡。\n\n「所以，這裡真的什麼都有。」晴晴喃喃地說，拿起筆記本，飛快地記錄。\n\n「而且，你們有沒有發現？」她抬起頭，「這裡的天空……好像比較藍。」\n\n林澈抬頭。真的。天空藍得很乾淨，像被水洗過一樣。\n\n三個人往六年一班的教室走去。\n\n教室的門開著。門口，站著一群孩子。\n\n兩班的人，第一次面對面。\n\n一開始，誰都沒有說話。大家你看我、我看你，好像在照鏡子。\n\n然後，對面的「達哥」先開口了。他大步走過來，拍拍阿達的肩膀：「你就是我喔？」\n\n阿達整個人僵住。他平常話最多，可是這一刻，他一個字都說不出來。\n\n達哥笑起來：「你怎麼這麼緊張？我們長得一模一樣耶，你應該要覺得很酷啊！」\n\n旁邊幾個 B 世界的同學也圍了過來，好奇地看著阿達：「哇，真的好像。」「可是他看起來好小喔。」「達哥，你以前是不是長這樣？」\n\n「什麼以前，我們同歲好嗎！」達哥笑罵回去，一群人笑成一團。\n\n阿達站在笑聲中間，忽然覺得自己好像變成了「配角」。\n\n在他自己的班，他是那個講笑話會被叫「安靜」的阿達。可是在這裡，大家都聽達哥的。\n\n另一邊，晴晴也遇到了對手。\n\n「你就是我？」對面的晴晴歪著頭看她，笑得很開心，「你怎麼看起來這麼嚴肅？」\n\n「我是班長。」晴晴回答，語氣有點硬。\n\n「班長？」對面的晴晴眨眨眼，「我不是班長耶。我們班班長是小陽。」\n\n她往旁邊一指。一個高高瘦瘦的男生正站在教室門口，跟一群同學勾肩搭背地聊天。講到一半，他忽然大笑起來，笑聲大到連走廊都聽得到。\n\n「那是趙小陽。」對面的晴晴說，「我們班的事情，都是他帶頭。他超會帶氣氛的。」\n\n晴晴看著那個笑聲宏亮的男生，又看看自己。\n\n同一個人，卻活在完全不一樣的位置上。\n\n她的筆記本上，默默地多了一行字：「B 世界：班長＝小陽。予晴（我）＝自由。」\n\n而林澈，站在原地，一直沒有動。\n\n因為他知道，那個人還沒有出現。\n\n「林澈！」\n\n一個聲音從教室裡傳來。\n\n林澈抬起頭。\n\n教室中央，那個「另一個林澈」正朝著他走過來。\n\n他走路的樣子跟林澈完全不一樣。他抬頭挺胸，步伐很大，經過的地方，同學會自動讓開一條路。\n\n而且，他在笑。\n\n「我一直在等你。」B 世界的林澈說。\n\n「等我？」林澈的聲音有點啞。\n\n「對啊。」B 林澈理所當然地說，「因為我早就知道，總有一天會看到你。」\n\n他指了指自己的胸口：「我這裡，一直有一個感覺——好像世界上還有另一個我，過著不一樣的生活。」\n\n林澈愣愣地看著他。\n\n他忽然發現一件事。B 林澈說話的時候，周圍的同學會停下來聽。大家看他的眼神，是「這個人很重要」的眼神。\n\n那種眼神，林澈從來沒有見過。至少，從來沒有人用那種眼神看過他。\n\n一種奇怪的感覺，從他的胸口慢慢升上來。酸酸的，熱熱的。\n\n不是討厭，也不是生氣。\n\n是羨慕。\n\n他羨慕得，差點說不出話來。\n\n「你怎麼了？」B 林澈關心地問。\n\n「沒事。」林澈低下頭，「我只是……覺得你們這裡，好像很好。」\n\nB 林澈歪著頭看了他兩秒，忽然笑了。\n\n「那你要不要——」他伸出手，「明天單獨來？我想跟你聊聊。」\n\n林澈看著那隻手。\n\n他想起阿達的話：「不能亂碰『另一個自己』。」\n\n就在他要開口的瞬間——\n\n「時間！」晴晴突然大喊，「快四點二十分了！」\n\n一群人瞬間慌成一團。\n\n三個人匆匆忙忙跟 B 世界的人道別，衝回禮堂。\n\n林澈跑在最後面。就在他踏進鏡子前，B 林澈追了上來，把一張摺好的小紙條塞進他的手裡。\n\n「明天見。」B 林澈對他眨眨眼。\n\n下一秒，林澈一頭撞進銀白色的光裡。\n\n等他再睜開眼睛，他已經回到舊禮堂了。晴晴和阿達站在旁邊，喘得上氣不接下氣。\n\n「呼……呼……剛剛好。」晴晴看著手錶，「四點十九分。」\n\n三個人靠在牆上，誰都沒有說話，只是笑。\n\n回家的路上，林澈一個人走在最後面。他攤開手心那張紙條。\n\n紙條上，是跟自己一模一樣的字跡，寫著兩行字：\n\n「明天，我想跟你說一件事。」\n\n「是關於『那句話』的事。」\n\n林澈停下腳步。\n\n海風從海堤那邊吹過來，把紙條吹得輕輕作響。\n\n他忽然想起——今天，是鏡子打開的第三天。\n\n而明天，就是第四天了。\n","rawContentEn":"# Chapter 04 — Us in the Parallel World\n\nSeptember 19th, Wednesday.\n\nThat morning, three people in Class 6-1 were especially off.\n\nChing-Ching forgot to get her communication notebook signed by her mom. A-Da snickered to himself three times during morning self-study and got punished with standing. And Lin Che kept staring out the window — not even the white-eyes bird he loved, perched in the banyan tree, could bring him back.\n\nBecause their hearts had all been left behind in that mirror in the old auditorium.\n\nDuring morning self-study, the classroom was noisy. Yu-Ching stood up and slapped her desk: \"Quiet! Morning self-study is supposed to be quiet!\"\n\nThe whole class went silent for exactly three seconds, then started buzzing again.\n\nA-Da told a joke. A few classmates laughed twice, then got glared down by Yu-Ching: \"Wu Da, quiet.\"\n\nA-Da rubbed his nose and sat down.\n\nLin Che watched all of it, and suddenly thought of the \"other A-Da\" in the mirror yesterday — the A-Da surrounded by everyone, called \"Da-ge.\"\n\n\"If only...\"\n\nHe quickly shook his head and tossed the thought away.\n\nAfter school, 3:50 p.m. The three of them gathered at the back of the classroom.\n\nChing-Ching spread open her notebook, like she was reading out a battle plan: \"First, let's confirm the rules. One, the mirror opens at 4:05 and closes at 4:20. Two, we absolutely have to be back before it closes. Three—\"\n\n\"Three, don't touch 'the other you.'\" A-Da cut in.\n\n\"How do you know that?\" Ching-Ching glared at him.\n\n\"I guessed.\" A-Da said it with total confidence. \"That's how it works in movies.\"\n\nLin Che took a pencil from his pocket. A blue body, with the words \"Haisheng Bookstore\" printed on it.\n\n\"Why did you bring a pencil?\" A-Da asked.\n\n\"...No reason.\" Lin Che gripped it a little tighter. \"I just felt like I should.\"\n\nAt four o'clock sharp, the three of them stood before the old auditorium's iron gate. This time, the gate was open.\n\nInside, the mirror had already begun to glow. Silvery-white ripples spread in rings, as if someone had lit a lamp beneath the water's surface.\n\nAnd on the other side of the mirror, a group of children was already standing there.\n\nThe people of World B had been waiting for them all along.\n\n\"They're here!\" the other Ching-Ching shouted. Her hair was in a ponytail today, her smile bright, her uniform pin on the right side of her chest.\n\n\"Let's go.\" Ching-Ching took a deep breath and walked toward the mirror first.\n\n\"Wait, wait, wait—\" A-Da suddenly grabbed her. \"If anyone should go first, it should be me, right? I'm braver.\"\n\n\"You're just louder,\" Ching-Ching said.\n\n\"That's a kind of bravery too!\"\n\nIn the end, A-Da went first. He stood in front of the mirror, squeezed his eyes shut, and yelled: \"Charge—!\"\n\nThen his whole body \"sank\" into the mirror's surface, like walking through a door with no door.\n\nLin Che watched A-Da's back disappear into the silvery-white light. He froze for a second, then hurried to follow.\n\nThe feeling of crossing over was hard to describe.\n\nIt was like stepping into a pool of cool water. First came a buzzing in his ears, then all sound vanished — he couldn't hear the wind, the cicadas, or even his own heartbeat.\n\nThen, plop.\n\nHe opened his eyes.\n\nHe was standing in the old auditorium.\n\nThe same dust, the same curtains, the same old mirror. But — the smell of the air was different. The old auditorium back home had a stuffy, moldy smell. This one smelled faintly of sunshine.\n\n\"Whoa—\" A-Da's voice came from up ahead. \"You guys, come out and look!\"\n\nThe three of them walked out of the auditorium.\n\nAnd then they all froze.\n\nThis was Haisheng Elementary.\n\nThe same red-brick walls, the same breezeway, the same playground. But—\n\n\"A slide!\" A-Da pointed behind the playground and yelled.\n\nBehind the playground stood a slide. A blue slide, looking a little old, yet shiny and bright, with a few younger kids lined up beside it, waiting their turn.\n\nLin Che froze. Behind their own school playground, there was only an empty lot.\n\nBut here, the slide stood right there.\n\n\"So this place really does have everything,\" Ching-Ching murmured, pulling out her notebook and writing quickly.\n\n\"And, did you guys notice?\" She looked up. \"The sky here... it seems bluer.\"\n\nLin Che looked up. It was true. The sky was so clean and blue it looked like it had been washed.\n\nThe three of them headed toward the Class 6-1 classroom.\n\nThe classroom door was open. In the doorway stood a crowd of children.\n\nThe two classes stood face to face for the first time.\n\nAt first, nobody spoke. Everyone looked at one another, as if gazing into a mirror.\n\nThen, the \"Da-ge\" from the other side spoke first. He strode over and patted A-Da on the shoulder: \"So you're me?\"\n\nA-Da froze completely. He was usually the biggest talker, but in this moment, he couldn't get out a single word.\n\nDa-ge laughed: \"Why are you so nervous? We look exactly the same — you should think that's cool!\"\n\nA few World B classmates crowded over too, looking at A-Da curiously: \"Wow, they really do look alike.\" \"But he seems so small.\" \"Da-ge, is this what you used to look like?\"\n\n\"What do you mean 'used to'? We're the same age, okay!\" Da-ge laughed and scolded back, and the whole group dissolved into laughter.\n\nStanding in the middle of the laughter, A-Da suddenly felt like he'd become a \"supporting character.\"\n\nIn his own class, he was the A-Da whose jokes got him told to \"be quiet.\" But here, everyone listened to Da-ge.\n\nOn the other side, Ching-Ching had met her match too.\n\n\"You're me?\" The other Ching-Ching tilted her head and looked at her, smiling happily. \"Why do you look so serious?\"\n\n\"I'm the class monitor,\" Ching-Ching answered, her tone a little stiff.\n\n\"Class monitor?\" The other Ching-Ching blinked. \"I'm not the class monitor. Our class monitor is Xiao-Yang.\"\n\nShe pointed to the side. A tall, slim boy stood at the classroom door, arms slung over classmates' shoulders as he chatted. Halfway through, he suddenly burst out laughing, so loud the sound carried all the way into the hallway.\n\n\"That's Zhao Xiao-Yang,\" the other Ching-Ching said. \"He's the one who leads everything in our class. He's great at setting the mood.\"\n\nChing-Ching looked at the loud-laughing boy, then at herself.\n\nThe same person, living in a completely different position.\n\nQuietly, a new line appeared in her notebook: \"World B: monitor = Xiao-Yang. Yu-Ching (me) = free.\"\n\nAnd Lin Che stood in place, unmoving the whole time.\n\nBecause he knew that person hadn't shown up yet.\n\n\"Lin Che!\"\n\nA voice came from inside the classroom.\n\nLin Che looked up.\n\nIn the center of the classroom, the \"other Lin Che\" was walking toward him.\n\nThe way he walked was completely different from Lin Che. Chest out, strides long — classmates automatically parted to make way as he passed.\n\nAnd he was smiling.\n\n\"I've been waiting for you,\" the Lin Che of World B said.\n\n\"For me?\" Lin Che's voice came out hoarse.\n\n\"Of course.\" B Lin Che said it like it was obvious. \"Because I always knew that one day I'd see you.\"\n\nHe pointed at his own chest: \"Right here, I've always had a feeling — like there's another me in this world, living a different life.\"\n\nLin Che stared at him blankly.\n\nHe suddenly noticed something. When B Lin Che spoke, the classmates around him would stop to listen. The way they looked at him was the way people looked at someone important.\n\nThat kind of look — Lin Che had never seen it before. At least, no one had ever looked at him that way.\n\nA strange feeling slowly rose from his chest. Sour, and warm.\n\nIt wasn't dislike, and it wasn't anger.\n\nIt was envy.\n\nHe was so envious he could barely speak.\n\n\"What's wrong?\" B Lin Che asked with concern.\n\n\"Nothing.\" Lin Che lowered his head. \"I just... think it seems really nice here.\"\n\nB Lin Che tilted his head and studied him for two seconds, then suddenly smiled.\n\n\"Then how about—\" He held out his hand. \"Come alone tomorrow? I want to talk to you.\"\n\nLin Che looked at that hand.\n\nHe remembered A-Da's words: \"Don't touch 'the other you.'\"\n\nJust as he was about to answer—\n\n\"Time!\" Ching-Ching suddenly shouted. \"It's almost 4:20!\"\n\nThe whole group instantly fell into a panic.\n\nThe three of them hurriedly said goodbye to the people of World B and dashed back to the auditorium.\n\nLin Che ran at the very back. Just before he stepped into the mirror, B Lin Che caught up and stuffed a folded slip of paper into his hand.\n\n\"See you tomorrow.\" B Lin Che winked at him.\n\nThe next second, Lin Che plunged headfirst into the silvery-white light.\n\nWhen he opened his eyes again, he was back in the old auditorium. Ching-Ching and A-Da stood beside him, gasping for breath.\n\n\"Huff... huff... just in time.\" Ching-Ching checked her watch. \"4:19.\"\n\nThe three of them leaned against the wall. None of them spoke. They just laughed.\n\nOn the way home, Lin Che walked alone at the very back. He unfolded the slip of paper in his palm.\n\nOn the paper was handwriting identical to his own, two lines:\n\n\"Tomorrow, I want to tell you something.\"\n\n\"It's about 'that sentence.'\"\n\nLin Che stopped walking.\n\nThe sea breeze blew in from the seawall, rustling the paper in his hand.\n\nHe suddenly remembered — today was the third day the mirror had opened.\n\nAnd tomorrow would be the fourth.\n"},{"id":5,"rawContent":"# 第 05 章　兩個林澈\n\n九月二十號，星期四。\n\n早自習的時候，林澈一直捏著口袋裡那張紙條。紙條已經被他的手指揉得發軟了。\n\n數學課，老師在講分數的除法。林澈的眼睛看著黑板，腦子裡卻在數鐘錶的秒針。\n\n放學。四點。還有一個下午。\n\n他從來沒有覺得，一天的課這麼長過。\n\n中午打掃時間，晴晴拎著拖把走過來。\n\n「你今天要自己進去？」她問，眼睛看著地板，語氣像在聊今天午餐吃什麼。\n\n「……對。」林澈愣了一下，「你怎麼知道？」\n\n「B 世界的林澈，昨天塞了紙條給你。」晴晴抬起頭，「我看到了。而且你今天一整天都魂不守舍，太好猜了。」\n\n林澈無話可說。\n\n晴晴把一張從筆記本上撕下來的紙塞給他。上面密密麻麻寫滿了她整理出來的規則。\n\n「四點二十分前回來。」她說，「還有——」\n\n「別亂碰『另一個自己』。」林澈接話。\n\n「你知道就好。」晴晴點頭，拎著拖把走了。\n\n阿達從走廊另一端跑過來，湊在林澈耳邊大喊：「要是遇到危險，你就大叫！我們在外面等你！」\n\n「小聲一點啦。」林澈被他嚇了一跳，卻忍不住笑了。\n\n四點零五分。舊禮堂裡，鏡子準時亮起。\n\n銀白色的光，一圈一圈地盪開，像在等他。\n\n林澈一個人站在鏡子前。他深吸一口氣，閉上眼睛，往前走了一步。\n\n涼涼的。耳鳴。然後，所有的聲音都消失了。\n\n再睜開眼睛時，他已經在 B 世界的舊禮堂裡。\n\n而 B 林澈，就站在鏡子前面，笑著看他。\n\n「你真的來了。」\n\n「你約的，不是嗎？」林澈小聲說。\n\nB 林澈笑了起來，領著他走出禮堂。\n\n這個世界的海聲國小，像披著一層溫暖的濾鏡。夕陽的光比他們那邊柔和，風裡的鹹味比他們那邊淡，連牆角的花，都開得比較大朵。\n\n他們經過操場後面的溜滑梯時，B 林澈忽然跳了上去，拍拍旁邊的位置：「坐啊。」\n\n林澈猶豫了一下，爬上去坐好。\n\n兩個一模一樣的男孩，並肩坐在藍色溜滑梯上，腳懸在半空，晃啊晃的。\n\n「你們昨天看到溜滑梯，全都嚇了一跳。」B 林澈說，「所以……在你們那邊，這裡沒有溜滑梯，對吧？」\n\n「嗯。」林澈點頭，「我們那邊，只有一片空地。」\n\n「在我們這邊，什麼都還在。」B 林澈說。\n\n林澈沒說話。他想起晴晴的紀錄：兩個世界的記憶，都開始「對不上」了。\n\n「你要跟我說的，是什麼事？」林澈問。\n\nB 林澈看著遠方的海，沉默了一會。\n\n「開學第三天，體育課。」他開口，「我們班在選大隊接力最後一棒。」\n\n「全班都低頭，沒人敢接。我也是。我從以前就……很安靜。」\n\n林澈愣住了：「你？安靜？」\n\nB 林澈笑了：「聽起來很不可思議吧？可是那天以前，我跟你一樣。坐在窗邊，不講話，不舉手，像教室裡的一片影子。」\n\n「然後呢？」林澈的聲音緊繃起來。\n\n「然後，劉老師走到我面前。」B 林澈說，「他問我：『你想試試看嗎？我看你可以。』」\n\n林澈的心臟，重重地跳了一下。\n\n這句話……他聽過。在夢裡。在舊禮堂的鏡子前。在他想不起來的那一天。\n\n「我接了最後一棒。」B 林澈繼續說，「我跑贏了。全班把我舉起來。從那天以後，我好像變成另一個人。」\n\n「不是變成。」B 林澈轉頭看他，眼睛亮亮的，「是終於敢做自己了。」\n\n林澈低下頭，看著自己的手。\n\n「你們班那天，也選最後一棒嗎？」B 林澈問。\n\n「應該有吧。」林澈說，「可是……我想不起來。」\n\n「想不起來？」\n\n「嗯。」林澈的聲音越來越小，「我只記得陽光很好，記得劉老師吹哨子。其他的……都是霧。」\n\nB 林澈看著他，忽然伸出手，握住了他的手。\n\n「說不定，你只是忘了。不是沒有。」\n\n就在兩隻手碰到的那一瞬間——\n\n世界，亮起來了。\n\n銀白色的光，從兩人的手之間炸開。\n\n然後，記憶像海浪一樣，撲了過來。\n\n林澈看見了。\n\n他看見陽光下，紅色跑道的另一端，接力棒滾到腳邊。他看見自己彎腰接住它，然後，跑。\n\n風在耳邊呼嘯。全班在旁邊尖叫：「林澈！林澈！林澈！」\n\n他看見終點線，看見自己第一個衝過去，看見自己被同學拋上天空。天空好藍，藍得發亮。\n\n那是 B 林澈的記憶。\n\n溫暖的、閃閃發光的、屬於另一個自己的記憶。\n\n畫面一轉。\n\n同樣的操場，同樣的陽光。可是顏色好像淡了一點。\n\n他看見自己站在跑道邊。低著頭。拳頭握緊，又鬆開。\n\n劉老師走到他面前。嘴巴一張一合，正在說話。\n\n可是，聲音像隔著一層厚厚的水。一個字，都聽不清楚。\n\n林澈拼命想聽清楚，想往老師的方向靠近，可是腳像被釘在地上，一動也不能動。\n\n就在畫面快要碎掉的前一刻，他彷彿看見，劉老師的嘴型，做出了三個字。\n\n三個字。\n\n是哪三個字？\n\n下一秒，他醒了。\n\n「哈啊——」\n\n林澈整個人往後彈開，手從 B 林澈的手裡抽出來。兩個人都跌坐在地上，大口大口地喘氣。\n\n「你……也看到了嗎？」B 林澈的聲音在抖。\n\n林澈點頭。他的背全濕了。\n\n「我看到你跑贏了。」林澈說，「全班把你拋起來。天空很藍。」\n\nB 林澈沉默了很久。\n\n「我看到你站在跑道邊。」他慢慢地說，「劉老師在跟你說話。可是……我也聽不到他說了什麼。」\n\n「你的記憶裡，那天缺了一塊。」B 林澈看著他，眼睛裡有心疼。\n\n林澈低頭，聲音很輕：「嗯。」\n\n兩人又沉默了一會。\n\n「沒關係。」B 林澈忽然站起來，拍拍褲子上的灰，朝他伸出手——但伸到一半，又縮了回去，改成比了一個大拇指。\n\n「我們會找到答案的。」他笑，「我們兩個一起。」\n\n林澈看著他。\n\n忽然，他覺得自己好像沒那麼羨慕了。或者說，不是「不羨慕」——是羨慕裡，多了一點別的東西。\n\n他也說不上來那是什麼。\n\n「四點二十分快到了。」最後，他只說出這一句。\n\nB 林澈陪他走回禮堂。\n\n鏡子前，兩人對望。\n\n「明天，我帶你認識我們班所有的人。」B 林澈說。\n\n「好。」\n\n林澈轉身，走進銀白色的光裡。\n\n回到自己的世界時，晴晴和阿達果然等在鐵門外。\n\n「遲到一分鐘！」阿達衝過來，「我們還以為你被鏡子吃掉了！」\n\n「你平安回來就好。」晴晴看著他的臉，皺眉，「你的臉色好白。發生什麼事了？」\n\n林澈笑了笑：「我好像……快想起來了。」\n\n回家的路上，夕陽把海染成金色。\n\n林澈一個人走著，腦子裡都是那三個字。\n\n劉老師的嘴型。三個字。會是什麼？\n\n他走到校門口的便利商店前面，下意識地抬頭看了一眼。\n\n就在那一秒，綠色的 7-ELEVEN 招牌，閃了一下。\n\n在那一閃裡，林澈清清楚楚地看見——招牌變成了藍色的，寫著四個字：\n\n「海聲書店。」\n\n只是一瞬間，就恢復了原狀。\n\n林澈站在原地，心跳得像打鼓。\n\n今天，是第四天。\n\n而世界，開始不對勁了。\n","rawContentEn":"# Chapter 05 — The Two Lin Ches\n\nSeptember 20th, Thursday.\n\nDuring morning self-study, Lin Che kept squeezing the slip of paper in his pocket. His fingers had already rubbed it soft.\n\nMath class. The teacher was explaining dividing fractions. Lin Che's eyes looked at the blackboard, but his mind was counting the clock's second hand.\n\nSchool dismissed. Four o'clock. One more afternoon.\n\nHe had never felt that a day of classes was so long.\n\nDuring the noon cleaning period, Ching-Ching walked over carrying a mop.\n\n\"You're going in alone today?\" she asked, eyes on the floor, her tone as casual as if she were discussing what they had for lunch.\n\n\"...Yes.\" Lin Che hesitated. \"How did you know?\"\n\n\"The Lin Che from World B slipped you a note yesterday.\" Ching-Ching looked up. \"I saw it. And you've been out of it all day — too easy to guess.\"\n\nLin Che had nothing to say to that.\n\nChing-Ching pressed a page torn from her notebook into his hand. It was covered in the rules she had compiled.\n\n\"Be back before 4:20,\" she said. \"And—\"\n\n\"Don't touch 'the other me,'\" Lin Che finished.\n\n\"As long as you know.\" Ching-Ching nodded and walked off with her mop.\n\nA-Da came running from the other end of the hallway and shouted right into Lin Che's ear: \"If anything dangerous happens, yell! We'll be waiting outside for you!\"\n\n\"Keep it down,\" Lin Che jumped, but couldn't help smiling.\n\n4:05 p.m. In the old auditorium, the mirror lit up on time.\n\nThe silvery-white light rippled outward in rings, as if waiting for him.\n\nLin Che stood before the mirror alone. He took a deep breath, closed his eyes, and stepped forward.\n\nCoolness. Ringing in his ears. Then all sound vanished.\n\nWhen he opened his eyes again, he was in the old auditorium of World B.\n\nAnd B Lin Che was standing right in front of the mirror, smiling at him.\n\n\"You really came.\"\n\n\"You asked me to, didn't you?\" Lin Che said quietly.\n\nB Lin Che laughed and led him out of the auditorium.\n\nHaisheng Elementary in this world seemed to be draped in a warm filter. The sunset light was softer than theirs, the salt in the wind fainter, and even the flowers by the wall bloomed larger.\n\nWhen they passed the slide behind the playground, B Lin Che suddenly hopped on and patted the spot next to him: \"Sit.\"\n\nLin Che hesitated, then climbed up and settled in.\n\nTwo identical boys sat side by side on the blue slide, feet dangling in the air, swinging.\n\n\"You all looked shocked when you saw the slide yesterday,\" B Lin Che said. \"So... over where you're from, there's no slide here, right?\"\n\n\"Mm.\" Lin Che nodded. \"Over there, it's just an empty lot.\"\n\n\"Over here, everything is still here,\" B Lin Che said.\n\nLin Che said nothing. He thought of Ching-Ching's notes: both worlds' memories were starting to \"not match up.\"\n\n\"So what is it you wanted to tell me?\" Lin Che asked.\n\nB Lin Che looked toward the distant sea and was quiet for a while.\n\n\"The third day of school. PE class.\" He began. \"Our class was picking the anchor leg for the relay.\"\n\n\"The whole class looked down. Nobody dared take it. Me neither. I was always... very quiet.\"\n\nLin Che froze: \"You? Quiet?\"\n\nB Lin Che laughed: \"Sounds unbelievable, right? But before that day, I was just like you. Sat by the window, never talked, never raised my hand — like a shadow in the classroom.\"\n\n\"Then what?\" Lin Che's voice tightened.\n\n\"Then Coach Liu walked up to me.\" B Lin Che said. \"He asked: 'Don't you want to give it a try? I think you can do it.'\"\n\nLin Che's heart thumped hard.\n\nThat sentence... he'd heard it before. In dreams. In front of the old auditorium's mirror. On that day he couldn't remember.\n\n\"I took the anchor leg,\" B Lin Che went on. \"I won. The whole class lifted me up. After that day, I seemed to become a different person.\"\n\n\"Not become,\" B Lin Che turned to look at him, eyes bright. \"It was finally daring to be myself.\"\n\nLin Che lowered his head and looked at his hands.\n\n\"Did your class pick an anchor leg that day too?\" B Lin Che asked.\n\n\"I think so.\" Lin Che said. \"But... I can't remember.\"\n\n\"Can't remember?\"\n\n\"Mm.\" Lin Che's voice grew softer. \"I only remember the sunshine was nice, and Coach Liu blowing his whistle. Everything else... is fog.\"\n\nB Lin Che looked at him, then suddenly reached out and took his hand.\n\n\"Maybe you just forgot. Not that it never happened.\"\n\nThe moment their two hands touched—\n\nThe world lit up.\n\nSilvery-white light burst from between their hands.\n\nThen, memories crashed in like ocean waves.\n\nLin Che saw it.\n\nHe saw the other end of the red track under the sun, the relay baton rolling to his feet. He saw himself bend down to grab it, and then — run.\n\nThe wind roared in his ears. The whole class screamed from the sidelines: \"Lin Che! Lin Che! Lin Che!\"\n\nHe saw the finish line. He saw himself sprint across it first. He saw himself being tossed into the sky by his classmates. The sky was so blue it gleamed.\n\nThose were B Lin Che's memories.\n\nWarm, sparkling memories, belonging to another him.\n\nThe scene shifted.\n\nThe same playground, the same sunshine. But the colors seemed a little paler.\n\nHe saw himself standing by the track. Head down. Fist clenched, then released.\n\nCoach Liu walked up to him. His mouth was moving, speaking.\n\nBut the sound was like it came through a thick layer of water. Not a single word was clear.\n\nLin Che strained desperately to hear, tried to move toward the teacher, but his feet were nailed to the ground — he couldn't budge.\n\nJust before the image shattered, he thought he saw Coach Liu's mouth form three words.\n\nThree words.\n\nWhat three words?\n\nThe next second, he came to.\n\n\"Ah—\"\n\nLin Che flung himself backward, pulling his hand free of B Lin Che's. Both of them collapsed to the ground, gasping for air.\n\n\"Did you... see it too?\" B Lin Che's voice trembled.\n\nLin Che nodded. His back was soaked with sweat.\n\n\"I saw you win,\" Lin Che said. \"The class tossed you up. The sky was so blue.\"\n\nB Lin Che was silent for a long time.\n\n\"I saw you standing by the track,\" he said slowly. \"Coach Liu was talking to you. But... I couldn't hear what he said either.\"\n\n\"In your memory, that day has a hole in it.\" B Lin Che looked at him, concern in his eyes.\n\nLin Che lowered his head and said very softly: \"Yeah.\"\n\nThe two of them fell silent again for a while.\n\n\"It's okay.\" B Lin Che suddenly stood up, dusted off his pants, and reached out his hand — but halfway through, he pulled it back and gave a thumbs-up instead.\n\n\"We'll find the answer.\" He smiled. \"The two of us, together.\"\n\nLin Che looked at him.\n\nSuddenly, he felt like he wasn't quite so envious anymore. Or rather — it wasn't that he was \"not envious.\" It was that something else had joined the envy.\n\nHe couldn't quite say what it was.\n\n\"It's almost 4:20,\" was all he managed to say in the end.\n\nB Lin Che walked him back to the auditorium.\n\nBefore the mirror, the two of them looked at each other.\n\n\"Tomorrow, I'll introduce you to everyone in our class,\" B Lin Che said.\n\n\"Okay.\"\n\nLin Che turned and walked into the silvery-white light.\n\nWhen he returned to his own world, Ching-Ching and A-Da were indeed waiting outside the iron gate.\n\n\"One minute late!\" A-Da rushed over. \"We thought the mirror ate you!\"\n\n\"The important thing is you got back safe.\" Ching-Ching looked at his face and frowned. \"You're really pale. What happened?\"\n\nLin Che smiled a little: \"I think I'm... starting to remember.\"\n\nOn the way home, the sunset dyed the sea gold.\n\nLin Che walked alone, the three words spinning in his head.\n\nCoach Liu's mouth. Three words. What could they be?\n\nWhen he reached the convenience store by the school gate, he instinctively looked up.\n\nAt that exact second, the green 7-ELEVEN sign flickered.\n\nIn that flicker, Lin Che saw it clearly — the sign had turned blue, and on it were four words:\n\n\"Haisheng Bookstore.\"\n\nJust for an instant, and then it was back to normal.\n\nLin Che stood rooted to the spot, his heart pounding like a drum.\n\nToday was the fourth day.\n\nAnd the world was starting to go wrong.\n"},{"id":6,"rawContent":"# 第 06 章　大人的世界\n\n九月二十一號，星期五。\n\n早上七點四十分，林澈在校門口停住了腳步。\n\n小七的招牌，在抖。\n\n不是風吹的那種晃。是像電視收訊不良一樣，一下是綠色的小七，一下是藍色的書店，來來回回地閃。\n\n可是，送小孩上學的家長、騎車經過的阿伯、走進店裡買報紙的老師，沒有一個人抬頭看。\n\n「他們看不到。」一個聲音從旁邊傳來。\n\n是晴晴。她站在林澈身後，揹著書包，盯著那塊招牌。\n\n「我站在這裡算過了。」她說，「從七點半到現在，招牌閃了十二次。沒有一個大人發現。」\n\n「為什麼？」林澈問。\n\n晴晴搖搖頭：「還不知道。不過，這也許是好事。」\n\n「好事？」\n\n「至少不會有人來把鏡子搬走。」她說。\n\n阿達從後面衝過來，頭髮翹得比平常更高：「早安！你們也看到那個了吧！」\n\n三個人並肩走進校門。\n\n早上的課，三個人都沒聽進去。\n\n數學課，林澈在課本角落畫了一面小小的鏡子。\n\n國語課，晴晴在桌墊下面繼續寫她的怪事紀錄。\n\n社會課，阿達折了一隻紙飛機，上面寫著「放學出動」。\n\n四點零五分。舊禮堂。鏡子準時亮起。\n\n三個人一起穿過鏡面。\n\n第三次了。林澈想。他已經開始習慣那陣涼涼的感覺。\n\nB 世界。今天來接他們的，是四個人。\n\nB 林澈、B 晴晴、達哥，還有一個笑聲很大的人——小陽。\n\n「歡迎光臨！」小陽張開雙臂，笑得像太陽，「歡迎來到另一個海聲國小！」\n\n「小聲一點，等下全校都聽到了。」B 晴晴笑著推他。\n\n晴晴看著小陽，又看看自己。這個世界，連班長都是笑著的。\n\n「走！帶你們逛學校！」小陽大手一揮。\n\n七個人浩浩蕩蕩地出發。\n\n第一站，六年一班教室。\n\n教室後面的佈告欄，貼滿了畫。有風景、有動物、有漫畫，五顏六色，滿到快要掉下來。\n\n「這些都是徐安安畫的！」達哥介紹，「她超會畫畫，是我們班的驕傲！」\n\n林澈想起 A 世界的安安——那個總是把畫本藏在抽屜最深處的女生。\n\n同樣的安安。不同的世界。\n\n第二站，教室後面的小農場。\n\n幾塊小小的田畦，種著蔥、空心菜和草莓。綠油油的一片，還有一隻橘色的貓躺在田埂上曬太陽。\n\n「我們班認養的！」B 晴晴說，「小陽是農場長。」\n\n「歡迎隨時來偷吃草莓！」小陽大笑，「啊不是，是來摘！」\n\n阿達看得兩眼發光。在 A 世界那裡，這是一大片水泥地。\n\n第三站，溜滑梯。\n\n阿達第一個衝上去，從上面滑下來，再衝上去，再滑下來。\n\n「這個一定要搬到我們那邊去！」\n\n達哥笑他：「喂，你這樣很像我小時候耶。」\n\n「我們同歲啦！」阿達大喊，兩個人在溜滑梯上追來追去。\n\n林澈看著他們，嘴角不知不覺往上揚。\n\n走到一半，林澈忽然問 B 林澈：「你們班的人，都看得到鏡子嗎？」\n\n「對啊。」B 林澈點頭，「全班二十六個人，每個都看得到。」\n\n「那大人呢？」\n\nB 林澈搖搖頭：「試過了。我們帶陳老師去看過——」\n\n「結果呢？」晴晴立刻湊過來。\n\n「老師說，那就是一面很髒的鏡子。」達哥接話，「還叫我們去拿抹布擦乾淨。」\n\n「我哥也來看過。」B 林澈說，「他今年國二，結果他什麼都看不到，還說我們在演電影。」\n\n晴晴低下頭，在筆記本上寫下：「大人看不見。國中生也看不見。」\n\n「為什麼只有我們看得見？」阿達問。\n\n大家你看看我，我看看你。\n\n「因為大人的眼睛裡，沒有魔法了！」小陽雙手叉腰，大聲宣布。\n\n大家笑成一團。雖然誰都知道，那不是真正的答案。\n\n第四站，校門口的書店。\n\n海聲書店。藍色的招牌，木頭的門框，門口的小黑板上寫著：「今日推薦：海邊的圖書館」。\n\n玻璃門推開，一個綁著頭巾的阿姨走出來。\n\n「哎喲，是你們這群小鬼。」她笑咪咪的，「今天放學比較早啊？」\n\n「老闆娘！」B 晴晴打招呼。\n\n老闆娘從圍裙口袋裡掏出一疊貼紙，一人一張：「來來來，都有都有。」\n\n林澈接過貼紙。是一張藍色的小鯨魚。\n\n他忽然想起自己口袋裡的那支鉛筆。藍色的筆身，上面印著「海聲書店」。\n\n一模一樣的四個字。\n\n「阿姨。」他脫口而出，「你們這裡，以前……有賣鉛筆嗎？」\n\n「鉛筆？」老闆娘想了想，「有啊，我們一直都有賣。你要嗎？送你一支！」\n\n林澈搖搖頭，握緊口袋裡的鉛筆：「不用了。我已經有一支了。」\n\n就在他們要離開的時候——\n\n招牌，閃了一下。\n\n藍色的「海聲書店」，一瞬間變成了綠色的 7-ELEVEN。\n\n雖然只有一秒鐘，可是七個孩子全都看到了。\n\n「剛剛……剛剛那是什麼？」達哥的聲音都變了。\n\nB 世界的孩子們愣在原地，臉色發白。\n\n「你們也開始了。」晴晴翻開筆記本，聲音很沉，「我們那邊的小七會閃成書店。你們這邊的書店，會閃成小七。」\n\n「這到底是怎麼回事……」B 晴晴喃喃。\n\n「覆蓋。」晴晴說，「兩個世界，正在互相覆蓋。」\n\n七個孩子站在書店門口，忽然都說不出話來。\n\n夕陽照在藍色的招牌上。海風吹過來，吹得手裡的小鯨魚貼紙輕輕地動。\n\n半晌，小陽第一個開口。\n\n「那我們還等什麼？」他大聲說，「趕快把原因找出來啊！」\n\n「可是要從哪裡找起……」阿達抓抓頭。\n\n「從『對不上的地方』找起。」晴晴說，「兩個世界所有對不上的地方，都是線索。」\n\nB 晴晴點頭：「我們也把我們這邊的怪事都記下來。兩邊一起查！」\n\n「兩班一起。」小陽伸出手。\n\n達哥把手疊上去。B 晴晴疊上去。B 林澈疊上去。\n\n三個人你看看我，我看看你。\n\n晴晴第一個伸出手，疊在最上面。然後是阿達。最後，是林澈。\n\n七隻手疊在一起，高高舉起——\n\n「喔——！」\n\n時間快到了。三個人衝回禮堂，跳進鏡子。\n\n回到 A 世界的舊禮堂時，三個人還喘著氣。\n\n「第五天。」晴晴翻開筆記本寫下，「覆蓋，是雙向的。」\n\n走出禮堂時，阿達忽然停下腳步，指著操場後面的空地。\n\n「你們看！」\n\n三個人同時轉頭。\n\n夕陽下，那片空地上，浮現出一圈淡淡的影子——\n\n一座溜滑梯的影子。\n\n影子很淡，像被水洗過的鉛筆痕跡。可是，清清楚楚，是一整座溜滑梯的形狀。\n\n「它……在過來。」林澈輕聲說。\n\n沒有人說話。\n\n海風吹過空空的操場。\n\n那圈影子，慢慢淡了下去。\n\n可是三個人心裡都明白——兩個世界之間的界線，正在變薄。\n\n而且，時間不多了。\n","rawContentEn":"# Chapter 06 — The World of Adults\n\nSeptember 21st, Friday.\n\nAt 7:40 in the morning, Lin Che stopped in front of the school gate.\n\nThe 7-Eleven sign was flickering.\n\nNot swaying in the wind. It was flickering like a TV with bad reception — one moment it was the green 7-ELEVEN, the next it was the blue bookstore, flashing back and forth.\n\nBut the parents dropping off their kids, the uncle riding past on his scooter, the teacher walking in to buy a newspaper — not one of them looked up.\n\n\"They can't see it.\" A voice came from beside him.\n\nIt was Ching-Ching. She stood behind Lin Che, backpack on, staring at the sign.\n\n\"I've been standing here counting,\" she said. \"From 7:30 until now, the sign has flickered twelve times. Not a single adult noticed.\"\n\n\"Why?\" Lin Che asked.\n\nChing-Ching shook her head: \"I don't know yet. But maybe that's a good thing.\"\n\n\"A good thing?\"\n\n\"At least no one will come and take the mirror away,\" she said.\n\nA-Da charged over from behind, his hair sticking up even higher than usual: \"Morning! You guys saw that too, right?\"\n\nThe three of them walked through the gate side by side.\n\nNone of them absorbed a single word of the morning's classes.\n\nIn math class, Lin Che drew a tiny mirror in the corner of his textbook.\n\nIn Chinese class, Ching-Ching kept writing in her strange-happenings log under her desk pad.\n\nIn social studies class, A-Da folded a paper airplane and wrote on it: \"Mission after school.\"\n\n4:05 p.m. The old auditorium. The mirror lit up right on time.\n\nThe three of them passed through the mirror together.\n\nThe third time, Lin Che thought. He was already getting used to that cool feeling.\n\nWorld B. Today, four people had come to meet them.\n\nB Lin Che, B Ching-Ching, Da-ge, and someone with a very loud laugh — Xiao-Yang.\n\n\"Welcome!\" Xiao-Yang spread his arms wide, smiling like the sun. \"Welcome to the other Haisheng Elementary!\"\n\n\"Keep it down, the whole school's going to hear you,\" B Ching-Ching laughed and shoved him.\n\nChing-Ching looked at Xiao-Yang, then at herself. In this world, even the class monitor was always smiling.\n\n\"Come on! Let's show you around the school!\" Xiao-Yang swung his arm in a grand gesture.\n\nThe seven of them set off in a bustling group.\n\nFirst stop: the Class 6-1 classroom.\n\nThe bulletin board at the back of the classroom was covered in drawings. Landscapes, animals, comics — a riot of colors, so full they were about to spill off.\n\n\"All of these were drawn by Hsu An-An!\" Da-ge introduced her. \"She's amazing at drawing — the pride of our class!\"\n\nLin Che thought of the An-An in World A — the girl who always hid her sketchbook in the deepest part of her desk drawer.\n\nThe same An-An. A different world.\n\nSecond stop: the little farm behind the classroom.\n\nA few small plots growing scallions, water spinach, and strawberries. A lush patch of green, with an orange cat sunbathing on the field ridge.\n\n\"Our class adopted it!\" B Ching-Ching said. \"Xiao-Yang is the farm manager.\"\n\n\"Feel free to steal strawberries anytime!\" Xiao-Yang laughed loudly. \"I mean — come pick them!\"\n\nA-Da's eyes shone. Over in World A, this was a big stretch of concrete.\n\nThird stop: the slide.\n\nA-Da was the first to charge up. He slid down, ran back up, slid down again.\n\n\"We have to move this thing over to our side!\"\n\nDa-ge laughed at him: \"Hey, you're acting just like me when I was little.\"\n\n\"We're the same age!\" A-Da yelled, and the two of them chased each other around the slide.\n\nLin Che watched them, and the corners of his mouth lifted without him noticing.\n\nHalfway through, Lin Che suddenly asked B Lin Che: \"Can everyone in your class see the mirror?\"\n\n\"Of course.\" B Lin Che nodded. \"All twenty-six of us can see it.\"\n\n\"What about adults?\"\n\nB Lin Che shook his head: \"We tried. We brought Teacher Chen to take a look—\"\n\n\"And?\" Ching-Ching leaned in immediately.\n\n\"The teacher said it was just a very dirty mirror,\" Da-ge cut in. \"And told us to go get a rag and wipe it clean.\"\n\n\"My brother came to look too,\" B Lin Che said. \"He's in eighth grade now. He couldn't see anything at all, and said we were acting in a movie.\"\n\nChing-Ching lowered her head and wrote in her notebook: \"Adults can't see it. Eighth graders can't see it either.\"\n\n\"Why can only we see it?\" A-Da asked.\n\nEveryone looked at each other.\n\n\"Because there's no more magic in grown-ups' eyes!\" Xiao-Yang put his hands on his hips and announced loudly.\n\nEveryone burst out laughing. Though they all knew that wasn't the real answer.\n\nFourth stop: the bookstore by the school gate.\n\nHaisheng Bookstore. A blue sign, a wooden door frame, and a little blackboard out front that read: \"Today's Recommendation: The Library by the Sea.\"\n\nThe glass door swung open, and an auntie with a kerchief on her head stepped out.\n\n\"Oh my, it's you little rascals.\" She beamed. \"School's out early today?\"\n\n\"Boss lady!\" B Ching-Ching greeted her.\n\nThe boss lady pulled a stack of stickers from her apron pocket, one for each of them: \"Here, here, there's enough for everyone.\"\n\nLin Che took his sticker. It was a small blue whale.\n\nHe suddenly remembered the pencil in his pocket. A blue body, with the words \"Haisheng Bookstore\" printed on it.\n\nExactly the same four words.\n\n\"Auntie,\" he blurted out, \"do you... do you sell pencils here?\"\n\n\"Pencils?\" The boss lady thought for a moment. \"We do — we've always sold them. Do you want one? I'll give you one for free!\"\n\nLin Che shook his head and gripped the pencil in his pocket: \"No need. I already have one.\"\n\nJust as they were about to leave—\n\nThe sign flickered.\n\nThe blue \"Haisheng Bookstore\" turned into a green 7-ELEVEN for a single instant.\n\nIt lasted only a second, but all seven children saw it.\n\n\"Wh... what was that just now?\" Even Da-ge's voice changed.\n\nThe World B children stood frozen, faces pale.\n\n\"It's starting on your side too.\" Ching-Ching opened her notebook, her voice heavy. \"Over on our side, the 7-Eleven flashes into a bookstore. On your side, the bookstore flashes into a 7-Eleven.\"\n\n\"What in the world is going on...\" B Ching-Ching murmured.\n\n\"Overwriting,\" Ching-Ching said. \"The two worlds are overwriting each other.\"\n\nStanding at the bookstore entrance, the seven children suddenly found themselves speechless.\n\nThe sunset fell on the blue sign. The sea breeze blew over, ruffling the little whale stickers in their hands.\n\nAfter a long moment, Xiao-Yang spoke first.\n\n\"Then what are we waiting for?\" he said loudly. \"Let's hurry up and find the cause!\"\n\n\"But where do we even start...\" A-Da scratched his head.\n\n\"With the places that don't match,\" Ching-Ching said. \"Every place where the two worlds don't line up is a clue.\"\n\nB Ching-Ching nodded: \"We'll write down all the strange things on our side too. Let's investigate together!\"\n\n\"Both classes, together.\" Xiao-Yang held out his hand.\n\nDa-ge stacked his hand on top. Then B Ching-Ching. Then B Lin Che.\n\nThe three of them glanced at each other.\n\nChing-Ching was the first to reach out and stack her hand on top. Then A-Da. And finally, Lin Che.\n\nSeven hands stacked together, raised high—\n\n\"Yay—!\"\n\nTime was almost up. The three of them dashed back to the auditorium and jumped into the mirror.\n\nBack in the old auditorium of World A, the three of them were still catching their breath.\n\n\"Day five.\" Ching-Ching opened her notebook and wrote: \"The overwriting goes both ways.\"\n\nAs they walked out of the auditorium, A-Da suddenly stopped and pointed at the empty lot behind the playground.\n\n\"You guys, look!\"\n\nAll three turned at the same time.\n\nIn the sunset, on that empty lot, a faint shadow had appeared—\n\nThe shadow of a slide.\n\nThe shadow was very faint, like a pencil sketch washed over by water. But it was unmistakably the shape of an entire slide.\n\n\"It's... coming over,\" Lin Che said softly.\n\nNo one spoke.\n\nThe sea breeze swept across the empty playground.\n\nThe shadow slowly faded away.\n\nBut all three of them understood — the boundary between the two worlds was growing thinner.\n\nAnd time was running out.\n"},{"id":7,"rawContent":"# 第 07 章　誰才是真的六年一班\n\n九月二十二號，星期六。\n\n早上九點，林澈被手機的震動聲吵醒。\n\n是群組訊息。阿達傳了一個貼圖，然後是晴晴：「下午兩點，校門口集合。我查到新東西。」\n\n「新東西？」\n\n晴晴：「到了再說。」\n\n下午兩點，三個人準時在校門口碰面。週六有管樂社練習，校門開著。\n\n路上，晴晴從書包裡拿出一個透明資料夾，裡面夾著一張泛黃的紙。\n\n「這是我拜託我表姊，從縣立圖書館的舊報紙資料庫印出來的。」她說。\n\n「三十年前的校刊專欄，標題叫〈禮堂的七日之謎〉。」\n\n林澈湊過去。泛黃的紙上，模糊地印著幾行字。\n\n「那年秋天，有學生說，舊禮堂的鏡子會發光。而且，連著七天。第八天，禮堂就因故停用了。從此，再也沒有開過。」\n\n「七日……」林澈喃喃。\n\n「如果這次也一樣，」晴晴說，「明天，就是最後一天。」\n\n阿達難得安靜，跟在後面踢著小石頭。\n\n走進校門時，林澈抬頭看小七的招牌。它還在閃，而且今天閃得更厲害了——綠色的招牌，已經有半分鐘整個變成藍色。\n\n「時間不多了。」晴晴說。\n\n舊禮堂。三個人推開鐵門。\n\n鏡子，已經開了。\n\n銀白色的光在鏡面上流動。可是現在是下午兩點半——平常四點五分才開。\n\n「提早了。」晴晴皺眉，記下時間。\n\n「裂痕也變長了。」林澈指著鏡子右下角。那道銀白色的裂痕，分出了兩條岔，像一道小小的閃電。\n\n三個人穿過鏡子。\n\nB 世界。今天，B 世界全班都到了。\n\n溜滑梯旁的草坪上，一群孩子或坐或站，看到他們三個，全都揮起手來。\n\n「他們來啦！」小陽大喊。\n\n一開始，氣氛很好。大家一起聊天、交換零食、比對兩個世界的不同。\n\n「你們的養樂多，跟我們的一樣嗎？」B 阿寶問。\n\n「一樣啊。不然咧？」阿達說。\n\n「喔——」B 阿寶拉長聲音，「可是你們連書店都沒有，好可憐。我們這裡，有書店、有農場、有溜滑梯，什麼都有。」\n\n氣氛瞬間冷了下來。\n\n「我們有過書店。」晴晴開口，語氣很硬。\n\n「有過？」B 阿寶聳聳肩，「現在沒有，就是沒有啊。你們的世界，好無聊。」\n\n阿達跳起來：「你說什麼？！」\n\n「我說事實啊。」B 阿寶不甘示弱，「你們學校後面光禿禿的，連溜滑梯都沒有，憑什麼說你們才是真的六年一班？」\n\n「我們就是真的！」阿達吼，「你們只是鏡子裡的假貨！」\n\n空氣瞬間凍結。\n\nB 世界的孩子們安靜下來，一個一個看過來。\n\n「你說誰是假貨？」達哥站出來，臉沉了下來。\n\n「你、你們啊！」阿達吼回去，「這整個世界，都是假的！」\n\n林澈看到 B 林澈的眼神，暗了一下。\n\n「夠了！」小陽大步走過來，站在阿達面前，「你們的世界才奇怪吧？又嚴肅又無聊，連笑都很少笑！我們才不可能是從那種世界分出來的！」\n\n「紀律就是紀律！」晴晴也站出來，跟小陽面對面，「我們才是真正的六年一班！」\n\n兩個班長，隔著三十公分，瞪著對方。\n\n兩邊的孩子也分成了兩邊，劍拔弩張。\n\n太陽不知不覺斜了。\n\n沒有人注意到時間。\n\n爭執越來越激烈。B 世界的孩子說 A 世界無聊；A 世界的三個孩子說 B 世界虛假。\n\n林澈想勸架，可是他的聲音太小，一下子就被淹沒。\n\n只有 B 林澈，從頭到尾沒有加入爭吵。他一直看著林澈。\n\n忽然，B 晴晴尖叫起來：「鏡子！鏡子在縮小了！」\n\n所有人回頭。\n\n舊禮堂裡透出的光，一明一滅，像一盞快熄滅的燈。銀白色的波紋，正一圈一圈往中間收縮。\n\n「快回去！」小陽大喊。\n\n三個人轉身往禮堂衝。\n\n跑到一半，阿達突然停下。\n\n「阿達！」晴晴回頭喊。\n\n阿達站在原地，低著頭。\n\n「回去幹嘛？」他的聲音悶悶的，「反正我們那邊，什麼都沒有。連書店都沒有。」\n\n林澈和晴晴同時愣住。\n\n鏡子的光越來越暗，波紋幾乎要縮成一個點。\n\n「你在說什麼傻話！」晴晴衝回去拉他。\n\n「我不回去！」阿達甩開她的手，眼眶紅了，「你們自己回去！我要留在這裡！這裡有溜滑梯！有書店！還有……還有被大家叫達哥的……」\n\n他的聲音卡住了。\n\n林澈看著他，忽然明白了。\n\n阿達不是真的想留下。他只是……覺得自己的世界，太差了。\n\n「阿達。」林澈走過去，站在他面前，「你記得嗎？」\n\n「記得什麼？」\n\n「校門口的小七招牌，會閃成書店。」林澈說，「你說，我們那邊什麼都沒有。可是招牌在閃，就表示書店沒有消失。」\n\n「它只是……還沒回來而已。」\n\n阿達愣愣地看著他。\n\n「而且，」林澈的聲音很輕，卻很穩，「我們三個都在這裡。如果你不回去，我們就少一個人了。」\n\n鏡子的光，暗到幾乎看不見了。\n\n「快！」晴晴抓住阿達的左手。\n\n林澈抓住阿達的右手。\n\n「走了！」三個人拔腿狂奔。\n\n就在波紋消失的前一秒——\n\n三個人一頭撞進鏡子裡。\n\n啪。\n\n舊禮堂。A 世界。\n\n三個人跌坐在地上，喘得說不出話來。\n\n過了很久，阿達才開口。他的聲音很小，小到幾乎聽不見。\n\n「對不起。」\n\n「沒有什麼好對不起的。」晴晴說，「如果你真的想留下，我們攔不住你。可是，你回來了。」\n\n她看著他：「所以，你的世界裡，一定有你想守護的東西。」\n\n阿達低著頭，沒有說話。可是他的嘴角，動了一下。\n\n林澈轉頭看向鏡子。\n\n鏡面恢復成老舊的樣子。右下角那道裂痕，變成了三道岔，像閃電，又像一棵細細的樹。\n\n晴晴翻開筆記本，寫下：\n\n「第六天。鏡子提早開、延後關。裂痕變成三岔。」\n\n她把筆記本合上，聲音很輕，卻像在宣布一件事：\n\n「明天，就是第七天了。」\n\n三個人沉默地走出禮堂。\n\n夕陽下，操場後面的空地上，那圈溜滑梯的影子，比昨天又深了一點。\n\n幾乎可以看見輪廓了。\n\n「喂。」阿達忽然說，「明天，我們去把小七的招牌照下來吧。」\n\n「為什麼？」\n\n「因為如果明天書店真的回來，」阿達抓抓頭，「至少要有人記得，它曾經是小七啊。」\n\n晴晴看著他，忽然笑了。\n\n林澈也笑了。\n\n海風吹過空空的操場，把那圈溜滑梯的影子，吹得輕輕搖晃。\n","rawContentEn":"# Chapter 07 — Which Is the Real Class 6-1?\n\nSeptember 22nd, Saturday.\n\nAt nine in the morning, Lin Che was woken by his phone vibrating.\n\nIt was a group chat message. A-Da had sent a sticker, followed by Ching-Ching: \"Meet at the school gate at 2 p.m. I found something new.\"\n\n\"Something new?\"\n\nChing-Ching: \"I'll tell you when we meet.\"\n\nAt two o'clock, the three of them met on time at the school gate. The school wind ensemble had practice on Saturdays, so the gate was open.\n\nOn the way, Ching-Ching pulled a clear plastic folder from her backpack. Inside was a sheet of yellowed paper.\n\n\"I had my cousin print this from the county library's old newspaper database,\" she said.\n\n\"A school newsletter column from thirty years ago, titled 'The Seven-Day Mystery of the Auditorium.'\"\n\nLin Che leaned over. Faintly printed on the yellowed paper were a few lines of text.\n\n\"That autumn, students said the mirror in the old auditorium glowed. And it did so for seven days in a row. On the eighth day, the auditorium was closed for certain reasons. And after that, it never opened again.\"\n\n\"Seven days...\" Lin Che murmured.\n\n\"If it's the same this time,\" Ching-Ching said, \"then tomorrow is the last day.\"\n\nA-Da was unusually quiet, kicking a small stone along behind them.\n\nAs they walked through the gate, Lin Che looked up at the 7-Eleven sign. It was still flickering, and even more violently today — the green sign was now turning entirely blue for half a minute at a time.\n\n\"Time is running out,\" Ching-Ching said.\n\nThe old auditorium. The three of them pushed open the iron gate.\n\nThe mirror was already open.\n\nSilvery-white light flowed across its surface. But it was 2:30 in the afternoon — normally it didn't open until 4:05.\n\n\"It's early,\" Ching-Ching frowned, jotting down the time.\n\n\"And the crack has gotten longer,\" Lin Che pointed at the lower-right corner of the mirror. The silvery-white crack had split into two branches, like a tiny lightning bolt.\n\nThe three of them passed through the mirror.\n\nWorld B. Today, the entire World B class had come.\n\nOn the lawn beside the slide, a crowd of children sat or stood around. When they saw the three, everyone waved.\n\n\"They're here!\" Xiao-Yang shouted.\n\nAt first, the mood was great. Everyone chatted, swapped snacks, and compared the differences between the two worlds.\n\n\"Is your Yakult the same as ours?\" B A-Bao asked.\n\n\"It's the same. What else would it be?\" A-Da said.\n\n\"Oh—\" B A-Bao drawled. \"But you guys don't even have a bookstore. So sad. Over here, we've got a bookstore, a farm, a slide — everything.\"\n\nThe atmosphere instantly cooled.\n\n\"We had a bookstore,\" Ching-Ching said, her tone hard.\n\n\"Had?\" B A-Bao shrugged. \"If it's not there now, it's not there. Your world is so boring.\"\n\nA-Da jumped up: \"What did you say?!\"\n\n\"I said the truth.\" B A-Bao didn't back down. \"The back of your school is all bare — you don't even have a slide. What makes you think you're the real Class 6-1?\"\n\n\"We ARE the real one!\" A-Da roared. \"You guys are just fakes in a mirror!\"\n\nThe air froze.\n\nThe World B children went quiet, one by one, looking over.\n\n\"Who are you calling a fake?\" Da-ge stepped forward, his face darkening.\n\n\"Y-you guys!\" A-Da shouted back. \"This whole world is fake!\"\n\nLin Che saw B Lin Che's eyes dim for a moment.\n\n\"Enough!\" Xiao-Yang strode over and stood in front of A-Da. \"Your world is the weird one! So serious and boring — you barely even laugh! There's no way WE branched off from a world like that!\"\n\n\"Rules are rules!\" Ching-Ching stepped forward too, standing face to face with Xiao-Yang. \"WE are the real Class 6-1!\"\n\nThe two class monitors stood thirty centimeters apart, glaring at each other.\n\nThe children on both sides split into two camps, tensions at a boiling point.\n\nBefore anyone knew it, the sun had begun to tilt.\n\nNo one noticed the time.\n\nThe argument grew more and more heated. The World B children said World A was boring; the three from World A said World B was fake.\n\nLin Che tried to break it up, but his voice was too small and was drowned out instantly.\n\nOnly B Lin Che never joined the quarrel from beginning to end. He kept looking at Lin Che.\n\nSuddenly, B Ching-Ching shrieked: \"The mirror! The mirror is shrinking!\"\n\nEveryone turned.\n\nThe light leaking from the old auditorium flickered on and off, like a lamp about to go out. The silvery-white ripples were contracting toward the center, ring by ring.\n\n\"Go back, quick!\" Xiao-Yang yelled.\n\nThe three of them turned and dashed toward the auditorium.\n\nHalfway there, A-Da suddenly stopped.\n\n\"A-Da!\" Ching-Ching shouted back.\n\nA-Da stood where he was, head down.\n\n\"Go back for what?\" His voice was muffled. \"Our side has nothing anyway. Not even a bookstore.\"\n\nLin Che and Ching-Ching froze at the same time.\n\nThe mirror's light grew dimmer and dimmer. The ripples had nearly shrunk to a single point.\n\n\"What nonsense are you saying!\" Ching-Ching ran back to pull him.\n\n\"I'm not going back!\" A-Da shook off her hand, his eyes reddening. \"You two go back yourselves! I'm staying! There's a slide here! A bookstore! And... and someone everyone calls Da-ge...\"\n\nHis voice caught in his throat.\n\nLin Che looked at him and suddenly understood.\n\nA-Da didn't really want to stay. He just... felt his own world was too lousy.\n\n\"A-Da.\" Lin Che walked over and stood in front of him. \"Do you remember?\"\n\n\"Remember what?\"\n\n\"The 7-Eleven sign by the gate keeps flashing into a bookstore,\" Lin Che said. \"You say our side has nothing. But if the sign is flashing, it means the bookstore hasn't disappeared.\"\n\n\"It just... hasn't come back yet.\"\n\nA-Da stared at him blankly.\n\n\"And besides,\" Lin Che's voice was soft but steady, \"all three of us are here. If you don't go back, we'll be one person short.\"\n\nThe mirror's light had dimmed almost to nothing.\n\n\"Now!\" Ching-Ching grabbed A-Da's left hand.\n\nLin Che grabbed A-Da's right hand.\n\n\"Run!\" The three of them bolted.\n\nJust one second before the ripples vanished—\n\nThe three of them plunged headfirst into the mirror.\n\nPlop.\n\nThe old auditorium. World A.\n\nThe three of them collapsed to the floor, too breathless to speak.\n\nIt was a long time before A-Da spoke. His voice was so small it was barely audible.\n\n\"Sorry.\"\n\n\"There's nothing to be sorry for,\" Ching-Ching said. \"If you really wanted to stay, we couldn't have stopped you. But you came back.\"\n\nShe looked at him: \"So there must be something in your world that you want to protect.\"\n\nA-Da kept his head down and said nothing. But the corner of his mouth twitched.\n\nLin Che turned to look at the mirror.\n\nThe mirror's surface had returned to its old, worn look. The crack in the lower-right corner had become three branches, like lightning — or like a thin little tree.\n\nChing-Ching opened her notebook and wrote:\n\n\"Day six. The mirror opened early and closed late. The crack has split into three.\"\n\nShe closed the notebook, and her voice was soft, yet sounded like an announcement:\n\n\"Tomorrow is the seventh day.\"\n\nThe three of them walked out of the auditorium in silence.\n\nUnder the sunset, on the empty lot behind the playground, the shadow of the slide was a little darker than yesterday.\n\nIts outline was almost visible now.\n\n\"Hey.\" A-Da suddenly spoke. \"Tomorrow, let's take a photo of the 7-Eleven sign.\"\n\n\"Why?\"\n\n\"Because if the bookstore really comes back tomorrow,\" A-Da scratched his head, \"at least someone should remember that it was once a 7-Eleven.\"\n\nChing-Ching looked at him and suddenly smiled.\n\nLin Che smiled too.\n\nThe sea breeze swept across the empty playground, ruffling the shadow of the slide ever so gently.\n"},{"id":8,"rawContent":"# 第 08 章　輪流回家\n\n晚上八點半，阿達騎著腳踏車，衝到學校門口。\n\n他被媽媽唸了一頓——下午回家時，他把外套忘在舊禮堂外面的榕樹上了。媽媽洗衣服洗到一半，發現少了一件外套，把他趕出家門去拿。\n\n就在他捏住煞車、準備停車的時候，整個人僵住了。\n\n舊禮堂的方向，有光。\n\n不是路燈的光。是銀白色的光，一明一滅，從禮堂的窗戶縫裡漏出來。\n\n阿達的心跳瞬間加速。他拿出手機，在群組裡傳：「你們快來學校！！鏡子怪怪的！！」\n\n還補了一張模糊的照片。\n\n二十分鐘後，三個人都到了。不只三個人——A 世界的阿寶看到訊息，也騎著車衝了過來。\n\n晴晴背著一個小背包，裡面裝著手電筒和筆記本。林澈還喘著氣，看得出來是用跑的。\n\n四個人推開鐵門，走進禮堂。\n\n鏡子沒有全開。銀白色的光只剩一道縫，像一扇沒關好的門。\n\n縫裡，隱約可以看到 B 世界。B 晴晴、達哥、小陽的臉，擠在那一線光裡，很焦急。\n\n「你們終於來了！」聲音很小，像從很遠的地方傳過來，「我們班的人不見了！」\n\n「誰不見了？」晴晴湊近。\n\n「阿寶！」達哥說，「就是下午跟你們吵架的那個阿寶！他從下午就沒有回來！」\n\n四個人的心同時一沉。\n\n「他……在我們這邊。」林澈忽然說。\n\n他指著禮堂的角落。黑暗中，一個小小的身影縮在椅子堆後面，微微發抖。\n\n是 B 世界的阿寶。\n\n「阿寶！」阿達衝過去。\n\nB 阿寶抬起頭。他的臉很髒，眼眶很紅，跟下午那個嗆人的樣子完全不一樣。\n\n「我……我只是想看看，你們的世界是不是真的那麼無聊……」他小聲說，「結果我迷路了。回來的時候，鏡子已經關了……」\n\n「你什麼時候過來的？」晴晴問。\n\n「下午……三點左右。」\n\n晴晴低頭看手錶。九點零二分。\n\n「六個小時了。」她的聲音很輕，臉色卻變了。\n\n「六個小時，會怎樣？」阿達問。\n\n晴晴還沒回答，A 阿寶已經走到了 B 阿寶面前。\n\n兩個一模一樣的人，站在黑暗的禮堂裡，看著對方。\n\n「你……你是？」B 阿寶的聲音在抖。\n\n「我是你。」A 阿寶瞪大眼睛，「你真的……真的跟我長得一樣……」\n\n忽然，B 阿寶喃喃地說了三個字。\n\n「養樂多。」\n\n同一秒，A 阿寶也開口：「養樂多。」\n\n兩個人同時愣住。\n\n「你怎麼知道我要說這個？」B 阿寶往後退了一步。\n\n「我不知道啊！」A 阿寶也嚇壞了，「是嘴巴自己動的！」\n\n晴晴翻開筆記本，手飛快地寫：「九點。兩個阿寶，同邊六小時。開始同步——同一時間，說出一樣的話。」\n\n「同步？」林澈問。\n\n「對。」晴晴抬起頭，眼睛在黑暗裡發亮，「兩個自己，待在同一個世界太久，就會開始同步。習慣、動作、說的話，都會互相靠攏。」\n\n「再更久呢？」阿達的聲音有點抖。\n\n晴晴沉默了一下。\n\n「還不知道。但是——」她看向禮堂外那片空地，「你們還記得溜滑梯的影子嗎？」\n\n「我懷疑，如果同步太久，其中一個人會變淡。像影子一樣，被另一個世界覆蓋。」\n\n禮堂裡，安靜得可怕。\n\n「所以，阿寶今天晚上一定要回去。」晴晴說。\n\n「可是鏡子已經關了！」A 阿寶急了，「要等明天……」\n\n「等不到明天。」晴晴說，「如果我們猜得對，再六個小時，也就是明天凌晨三點——他可能就會開始變淡。」\n\n「那怎麼辦？」阿達抓頭。\n\n林澈看著那面鏡子。銀白色的細縫，還在微微地閃。\n\n「鏡子沒有全關。」他說。\n\n所有人都看向他。\n\n「今天鏡子開得特別久。」林澈慢慢地說，「說不定，它只是『縮小』，不是『關上』。如果……」\n\n「如果有看得見的孩子在，它就會張開。」晴晴接過他的話，「像每天下午那樣。」\n\n兩個人對視一眼。\n\n「試試看吧。」晴晴站起來，走到鏡子前面。\n\n五個孩子圍在鏡子前面。\n\n一開始，什麼都沒有發生。細縫還是細縫，光還是那麼淡。\n\n過了一分鐘。\n\n「拜託……」B 阿寶小聲說。他的眼眶又紅了，「拜託，讓我回家。」\n\n忽然，細縫亮了一下。\n\n接著，鏡面上的灰，一圈一圈地散開。那道縫，慢慢地、慢慢地，張大了一點點。\n\n只能容得下一個人的側身。\n\n縫的另一邊，B 世界的孩子們全都擠了過來。\n\n「阿寶！阿寶！」B 晴晴的聲音從縫裡傳出來，帶著哭腔。\n\n「快！」晴晴喊。\n\nB 阿寶往前走了一步，又停下來。\n\n他轉過頭，看向 A 阿寶。\n\n「對不起。」他說，「下午……我不應該說那些話。你們的世界，一點都不無聊。」\n\nA 阿寶看著他，忽然笑了。\n\n「我原諒你。」他說，「誰叫你是另一個我。而且——」\n\n他頓了一下：「其實我也覺得，我們學校後面，真的很空。」\n\n兩個阿寶同時笑了。\n\n然後，B 阿寶側過身子，擠進那道縫裡。\n\n就在他通過的瞬間，縫裡的光猛地一亮，然後——\n\n關上了。\n\n鏡面恢復黑暗。只有右下角那道裂痕，還在一閃一閃。\n\n「過去了嗎？」阿達小聲問。\n\n沒人回答。大家擠到鏡子前面，拼命往裡看。\n\n忽然，鏡面微微亮了一下。B 晴晴的聲音傳過來，又小又模糊：\n\n「他過來了！他過來了！」\n\n所有人鬆了一大口氣。\n\n四個孩子坐在地上，誰都沒有說話。過了一會，阿達先笑了。\n\n「我們剛才，好像在拍電影。」\n\n大家都笑了。笑著笑著，眼睛卻有點濕。\n\n晴晴翻開筆記本，把規則一項一項寫下來：\n\n「規則一：鏡子，只有小孩看得見。」\n\n「規則二：兩個自己，同邊超過六小時，會同步。」\n\n「規則三：疑似超過十二小時，會被覆蓋。」\n\n「規則四：兩班必須輪流。不能讓任何一對自己，在同一個世界待超過三小時。」\n\n她把寫滿規則的紙撕下來，貼在鏡子旁邊。\n\n「這是『輪班表』。」她說，「明天鏡子一開，對面的人第一眼就會看到。」\n\n「明天，是最後一天。」晴晴的聲音很輕，卻很穩，「也是第七天。」\n\n四個人走出禮堂，準備回家。\n\n走到門口的瞬間，林澈忽然停下。\n\n「你們看。」\n\n三個人順著他的目光看過去。\n\n那道銀白色的裂痕，不只長在鏡子上。\n\n它已經從鏡子的木框，爬到了牆上。一道細細的、發著微光的紋路，像一道小小的閃電，沿著牆壁，往上蔓延。\n\n四個人仰著頭，看著那道裂紋，誰都說不出話來。\n\n「世界……」阿達的聲音發抖，「世界在裂開。」\n\n海風吹過夜晚的校園，吹得舊禮堂的窗戶嘎嘎作響。\n\n裂紋在月光下，靜靜地發著光。\n\n第七天，就要來了。\n","rawContentEn":"# Chapter 08 — Taking Turns to Go Home\n\nAt 8:30 in the evening, A-Da pedaled his bike and raced up to the school gate.\n\nHe'd gotten an earful from his mom — when he came home that afternoon, he'd left his jacket on the banyan tree outside the old auditorium. His mom was halfway through the laundry when she noticed a jacket was missing, and chased him out the door to go fetch it.\n\nJust as he squeezed the brakes to stop, he froze completely.\n\nFrom the direction of the old auditorium, there was light.\n\nNot streetlight. It was silvery-white light, flickering on and off, leaking through the cracks of the auditorium windows.\n\nA-Da's heart instantly began to race. He grabbed his phone and messaged the group chat: \"You guys, come to school now!! Something's wrong with the mirror!!\"\n\nHe even attached a blurry photo.\n\nTwenty minutes later, all three of them had arrived. More than three, actually — A-Bao of World A saw the message and came racing over on his bike too.\n\nChing-Ching carried a small backpack containing a flashlight and her notebook. Lin Che was still panting — clearly he'd run the whole way.\n\nThe four of them pushed open the iron gate and walked into the auditorium.\n\nThe mirror wasn't fully open. Only a slit of silvery-white light remained, like a door that hadn't been shut properly.\n\nThrough the slit, World B was faintly visible. The faces of B Ching-Ching, Da-ge, and Xiao-Yang crowded into that thin line of light, full of worry.\n\n\"You finally came!\" The voice was tiny, as if traveling from far away. \"Someone from our class is missing!\"\n\n\"Who's missing?\" Ching-Ching leaned closer.\n\n\"A-Bao!\" Da-ge said. \"The A-Bao who argued with you this afternoon! He hasn't come back since then!\"\n\nAll four hearts sank at once.\n\n\"He's... on our side,\" Lin Che suddenly said.\n\nHe pointed at the corner of the auditorium. In the darkness, a small figure huddled behind a stack of chairs, shivering slightly.\n\nIt was the A-Bao of World B.\n\n\"A-Bao!\" A-Da rushed over.\n\nB A-Bao looked up. His face was smudged, his eyes red — completely different from the provoking look he'd worn that afternoon.\n\n\"I... I just wanted to see if your world was really that boring...\" he said in a small voice. \"But I got lost. By the time I came back, the mirror had already closed...\"\n\n\"When did you come over?\" Ching-Ching asked.\n\n\"This afternoon... around three.\"\n\nChing-Ching looked down at her watch. 9:02 p.m.\n\n\"Six hours,\" she said, her voice soft but her face changing color.\n\n\"What happens at six hours?\" A-Da asked.\n\nBefore Ching-Ching could answer, A A-Bao had already walked up to B A-Bao.\n\nTwo identical people stood in the dark auditorium, looking at each other.\n\n\"Y-you... who are you?\" B A-Bao's voice trembled.\n\n\"I'm you.\" A A-Bao's eyes went wide. \"You really... really look exactly like me...\"\n\nSuddenly, B A-Bao murmured three words.\n\n\"Yakult.\"\n\nAt the exact same second, A A-Bao opened his mouth too: \"Yakult.\"\n\nBoth of them froze at the same time.\n\n\"How did you know I was going to say that?\" B A-Bao stepped back.\n\n\"I don't know!\" A A-Bao was frightened too. \"My mouth moved on its own!\"\n\nChing-Ching opened her notebook and wrote quickly: \"9 p.m. Two A-Baos, six hours on the same side. Sync has begun — they spoke the same words at the same time.\"\n\n\"Sync?\" Lin Che asked.\n\n\"Yes.\" Ching-Ching looked up, her eyes bright in the darkness. \"When two selves stay in the same world for too long, they start to sync. Habits, movements, words — they all start converging.\"\n\n\"And if it goes on even longer?\" A-Da's voice shook a little.\n\nChing-Ching was silent for a moment.\n\n\"I don't know yet. But—\" She looked toward the empty lot outside the auditorium. \"Do you remember the shadow of the slide?\"\n\n\"I suspect that if the sync goes on too long, one of them will grow faint. Like a shadow, overwritten by the other world.\"\n\nThe auditorium fell terribly silent.\n\n\"So A-Bao has to go back tonight,\" Ching-Ching said.\n\n\"But the mirror is already closed!\" A A-Bao panicked. \"We'd have to wait until tomorrow...\"\n\n\"He can't wait until tomorrow.\" Ching-Ching said. \"If we're right, in another six hours — three a.m. — he might start to fade.\"\n\n\"Then what do we do?\" A-Da scratched his head.\n\nLin Che looked at the mirror. The silvery-white slit was still faintly pulsing.\n\n\"The mirror didn't close all the way,\" he said.\n\nEveryone turned to look at him.\n\n\"Today the mirror stayed open especially long,\" Lin Che said slowly. \"Maybe it only 'shrank,' and didn't 'close.' What if...\"\n\n\"If there are children who can see it around, it will open.\" Ching-Ching finished his thought. \"Like it does every afternoon.\"\n\nThe two of them exchanged a glance.\n\n\"Let's try it.\" Ching-Ching stood up and walked over to the mirror.\n\nThe five children gathered in front of the mirror.\n\nAt first, nothing happened. The slit stayed a slit, and the light stayed faint.\n\nA minute passed.\n\n\"Please...\" B A-Bao whispered. His eyes reddened again. \"Please, let me go home.\"\n\nSuddenly, the slit brightened.\n\nThen the dust on the mirror's surface parted in rings. The slit slowly, slowly widened a little.\n\nJust enough for one person to squeeze through sideways.\n\nOn the other side of the slit, all the children of World B crowded in.\n\n\"A-Bao! A-Bao!\" B Ching-Ching's voice came through the slit, choked with tears.\n\n\"Hurry!\" Ching-Ching shouted.\n\nB A-Bao took a step forward, then stopped.\n\nHe turned and looked at A A-Bao.\n\n\"I'm sorry,\" he said. \"This afternoon... I shouldn't have said those things. Your world isn't boring at all.\"\n\nA A-Bao looked at him and suddenly smiled.\n\n\"I forgive you,\" he said. \"You're another me, after all. And—\"\n\nHe paused: \"Honestly, I think the back of our school is really empty too.\"\n\nThe two A-Baos laughed at the same time.\n\nThen B A-Bao turned sideways and squeezed into the slit.\n\nThe instant he passed through, the light in the slit flared bright, and then—\n\nIt closed.\n\nThe mirror's surface returned to darkness. Only the crack in the lower-right corner still pulsed faintly.\n\n\"Did he make it?\" A-Da whispered.\n\nNo one answered. Everyone crowded up to the mirror, straining to look inside.\n\nSuddenly, the mirror's surface glowed faintly. B Ching-Ching's voice came through, small and blurry:\n\n\"He made it! He's here!\"\n\nEveryone let out a huge sigh of relief.\n\nThe four children sat on the floor, none of them speaking. After a while, A-Da laughed first.\n\n\"We were like a movie just now.\"\n\nEveryone laughed. And as they laughed, their eyes grew a little wet.\n\nChing-Ching opened her notebook and wrote the rules down one by one:\n\n\"Rule One: Only children can see the mirror.\"\n\n\"Rule Two: Two selves, together on the same side for over six hours, will sync.\"\n\n\"Rule Three: Suspected — over twelve hours, one will be overwritten.\"\n\n\"Rule Four: The two classes must take turns. No pair of selves may stay in the same world for more than three hours.\"\n\nShe tore out the page of rules and taped it beside the mirror.\n\n\"This is the 'rotation schedule,'\" she said. \"Tomorrow, the moment the mirror opens, the people on the other side will see it first thing.\"\n\n\"Tomorrow is the last day,\" Ching-Ching said, her voice soft but steady. \"The seventh day.\"\n\nThe four children walked out of the auditorium, ready to head home.\n\nAt the doorway, Lin Che suddenly stopped.\n\n\"Look.\"\n\nThe other three followed his gaze.\n\nThe silvery-white crack wasn't only on the mirror anymore.\n\nIt had climbed from the mirror's wooden frame onto the wall. A thin line of faintly glowing light, like a tiny bolt of lightning, spreading upward along the wall.\n\nThe four of them tilted their heads up and stared at the crack. None of them could say a word.\n\n\"The world...\" A-Da's voice trembled. \"The world is cracking.\"\n\nThe sea breeze swept through the night campus, rattling the old auditorium's windows.\n\nThe crack glowed quietly in the moonlight.\n\nThe seventh day was about to arrive.\n"}];
